1
00:02:17,768 --> 00:02:21,204
(Day Eight, Morning)

2
00:02:22,106 --> 00:02:27,043
(The first awake: Superman)

3
00:02:38,756 --> 00:02:41,658
Nangja's usually the second one up,

4
00:02:41,993 --> 00:02:44,227
but she's not here, so it's you.

5
00:02:44,562 --> 00:02:46,629
Right. We were usually...

6
00:02:46,697 --> 00:02:48,898
the first three awake.

7
00:02:50,501 --> 00:02:52,068
- How about some fresh air?
- Sounds good.

8
00:02:53,771 --> 00:02:55,505
If someone gets evicted today,

9
00:02:55,506 --> 00:02:57,540
we planned to kick out the betrayer.

10
00:02:57,541 --> 00:02:58,407
Right.

11
00:02:58,408 --> 00:03:01,277
So I thought...

12
00:03:01,713 --> 00:03:04,147
when I was one
of the four eviction candidates...

13
00:03:04,715 --> 00:03:06,683
there was no tangible evidence...

14
00:03:06,684 --> 00:03:07,884
about me.

15
00:03:07,885 --> 00:03:09,752
But they still said,

16
00:03:09,753 --> 00:03:12,488
"We should just kill one of these four."

17
00:03:12,489 --> 00:03:14,957
- Right.
- Now, look.

18
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:16,759
It's just "as long as it isn't me."

19
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:19,529
For example, those without immunity...

20
00:03:19,997 --> 00:03:21,798
naturally make demands.

21
00:03:21,799 --> 00:03:22,698
And put on pressure.

22
00:03:22,699 --> 00:03:26,369
It just feels like a witch hunt.

23
00:03:26,937 --> 00:03:30,173
They said they'd create
a society safe for all,

24
00:03:30,775 --> 00:03:32,609
but when you're personally involved,

25
00:03:33,744 --> 00:03:35,912
it's hard if you haven't experienced it.

26
00:03:35,913 --> 00:03:37,981
They've all gone back on their word.

27
00:03:37,982 --> 00:03:41,551
I was drawn to the idea of finding
a way for everyone to survive,

28
00:03:41,552 --> 00:03:43,319
but it's easier said than done.

29
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:44,887
But in reality,

30
00:03:44,888 --> 00:03:47,957
it's the people trying hardest
to expose the spy...

31
00:03:47,958 --> 00:03:50,760
who keep getting suspected first.

32
00:03:50,761 --> 00:03:52,995
Now, keeping quiet is
the only safe option.

33
00:03:52,996 --> 00:03:54,897
You have to say nothing,
make no arguments,

34
00:03:54,898 --> 00:03:57,433
and just keep quiet to stay safe.

35
00:03:57,434 --> 00:03:59,168
Which honestly,

36
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:01,004
I'm finding hard to accept.

37
00:04:01,105 --> 00:04:03,106
That's what I couldn't understand.

38
00:04:03,107 --> 00:04:05,241
To be honest,
I have no reason to work hard today.

39
00:04:05,242 --> 00:04:07,243
Someone's going to be evicted today.

40
00:04:07,244 --> 00:04:09,212
From my perspective,
I'm starting the day...

41
00:04:09,213 --> 00:04:10,980
already sentenced to death.

42
00:04:10,981 --> 00:04:13,883
But I don't think it will be
a team game today.

43
00:04:13,884 --> 00:04:15,752
If it's a solo mission, then I think...

44
00:04:15,753 --> 00:04:18,288
Anyway, if everyone decides together,

45
00:04:18,289 --> 00:04:21,257
I'm number one on the chopping block,
and you're number two.

46
00:04:24,061 --> 00:04:26,596
Let's say the prize money on the line...

47
00:04:27,231 --> 00:04:29,265
were to increase tenfold...

48
00:04:29,266 --> 00:04:30,367
Then this would be...

49
00:04:30,368 --> 00:04:32,468
- A much bigger deal, right?
- Definitely.

50
00:04:32,469 --> 00:04:33,469
Of course.

51
00:04:33,738 --> 00:04:36,406
We all came here with different goals,

52
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:39,308
but once money is on the line,

53
00:04:39,744 --> 00:04:40,844
everyone's focus shifts to it.

54
00:04:40,845 --> 00:04:41,778
Right.

55
00:04:41,779 --> 00:04:44,081
Maybe you can only talk about fairness...

56
00:04:44,082 --> 00:04:46,183
when you have nothing to lose.

57
00:04:46,984 --> 00:04:48,551
Don't you have...

58
00:04:49,253 --> 00:04:51,588
Hi!

59
00:04:52,623 --> 00:04:54,290
Seriously.

60
00:04:54,291 --> 00:04:55,959
It's not exactly a good morning.

61
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:58,027
- It doesn't feel good.
- For you too?

62
00:04:58,362 --> 00:05:00,763
I still think the spy is up to something.

63
00:05:01,265 --> 00:05:03,166
People are starting to wake up.

64
00:05:04,007 --> 00:05:05,301
Got anything important to say?

65
00:05:05,569 --> 00:05:07,237
What's important is figuring out...

66
00:05:07,238 --> 00:05:09,071
how I can avoid being
public enemy number one.

67
00:05:09,072 --> 00:05:10,606
I have to convince everyone.

68
00:05:10,607 --> 00:05:11,807
And it won't be easy.

69
00:05:12,343 --> 00:05:13,543
The Reporter's question yesterday.

70
00:05:13,544 --> 00:05:15,578
Who came up with that idea?

71
00:05:15,579 --> 00:05:17,380
- Benjamin.
- It came...

72
00:05:17,381 --> 00:05:18,848
from him wondering, "Why?"

73
00:05:19,049 --> 00:05:19,915
Look at that.

74
00:05:21,685 --> 00:05:25,354
(Benjamin showing
a new side of himself last night)

75
00:05:25,738 --> 00:05:27,140
The spy committed murder.

76
00:05:27,224 --> 00:05:29,992
So what's the point
of eliminating them now?

77
00:05:30,694 --> 00:05:34,263
He was so different
from how calm he was before.

78
00:05:34,425 --> 00:05:35,424
He didn't seem like himself.

79
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:39,102
Right. He really went
after the traitor yesterday.

80
00:05:39,103 --> 00:05:41,137
So I played along...

81
00:05:41,138 --> 00:05:44,574
since I was the one
who first used the word traitor.

82
00:05:44,575 --> 00:05:46,342
And you have to keep doing it.

83
00:05:46,343 --> 00:05:49,679
I played along...

84
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:51,648
because it's one of my field's tactics.

85
00:05:51,882 --> 00:05:53,516
It's called "whataboutism."

86
00:05:54,752 --> 00:05:56,786
It's something we're good at.

87
00:05:56,987 --> 00:06:00,556
When there's a problem over here,
we highlight another problem and ask,

88
00:06:00,891 --> 00:06:03,593
"Hey, what about this?"

89
00:06:03,594 --> 00:06:04,460
That's what we do.

90
00:06:04,461 --> 00:06:06,362
That is a good tactic.

91
00:06:06,363 --> 00:06:07,864
That's whataboutism.

92
00:06:07,865 --> 00:06:08,898
We politicians use it a lot.

93
00:06:09,466 --> 00:06:11,434
People have started...

94
00:06:11,435 --> 00:06:14,270
openly saying Benjamin seemed strange.

95
00:06:14,271 --> 00:06:17,073
Everyone had their own slight suspicions,

96
00:06:17,074 --> 00:06:19,975
but now everyone seems to be agreeing.

97
00:06:20,177 --> 00:06:22,111
Everyone picked up on different signs.

98
00:06:22,112 --> 00:06:23,446
I noticed some things too.

99
00:06:23,681 --> 00:06:25,115
You'll never admit you're the spy, right?

100
00:06:25,383 --> 00:06:26,516
The spy?

101
00:06:26,817 --> 00:06:28,184
No way.

102
00:06:28,185 --> 00:06:30,920
If he's that good at lying,
there's no way to tell for sure.

103
00:06:31,188 --> 00:06:34,290
But I think he isn't because...

104
00:06:34,291 --> 00:06:36,659
He's just a little shady.

105
00:06:40,865 --> 00:06:42,934
Now that we know...

106
00:06:42,935 --> 00:06:45,235
I'm the one with the most money,

107
00:06:45,236 --> 00:06:46,703
if it comes down to one-on-one,

108
00:06:46,704 --> 00:06:49,272
I think I'm the most likely
to be attacked.

109
00:06:49,473 --> 00:06:50,440
But let's see.

110
00:06:50,774 --> 00:06:52,308
Like taking revenge,

111
00:06:52,309 --> 00:06:53,309
"I'll never forgive the traitor."

112
00:06:53,310 --> 00:06:54,377
It could go like that.

113
00:06:55,246 --> 00:06:57,514
But that's just a hunch.

114
00:06:57,515 --> 00:06:59,249
We could suspect anyone.

115
00:07:00,017 --> 00:07:01,852
- Right.
- So I had my suspicions too.

116
00:07:01,853 --> 00:07:03,286
I kept them in the back of my mind...

117
00:07:03,287 --> 00:07:05,421
and watched those people closely.

118
00:07:05,422 --> 00:07:06,856
But yesterday,

119
00:07:06,991 --> 00:07:09,325
one person seemed the strangest.

120
00:07:09,593 --> 00:07:11,327
Someone whose pattern kept
changing strangely.

121
00:07:11,595 --> 00:07:13,462
Just like the person
who spoke most harshly...

122
00:07:13,463 --> 00:07:15,031
about the traitor.

123
00:07:22,139 --> 00:07:23,005
Everyone's up.

124
00:07:25,643 --> 00:07:28,144
I'm acutely aware that people are...

125
00:07:28,379 --> 00:07:29,312
suspecting me.

126
00:07:29,513 --> 00:07:32,348
As soon as I enter the room,

127
00:07:32,683 --> 00:07:34,183
they stop talking.

128
00:07:34,184 --> 00:07:36,219
There are some obvious signs like that,

129
00:07:36,921 --> 00:07:38,021
so I strongly believe...

130
00:07:38,322 --> 00:07:40,056
they're trying to eliminate me.

131
00:07:42,459 --> 00:07:44,393
I was thinking earlier...

132
00:07:44,561 --> 00:07:46,529
that the people who are trusting others...

133
00:07:46,530 --> 00:07:49,532
for no valid reason might be
the most dangerous.

134
00:07:49,734 --> 00:07:50,834
And they rule out those people.

135
00:07:50,835 --> 00:07:52,001
- Right.
- Exactly.

136
00:07:52,202 --> 00:07:54,103
Maybe I'm trying to be too objective,

137
00:07:54,638 --> 00:07:56,573
but I wasn't here from the beginning,

138
00:07:57,207 --> 00:07:59,175
so it's hard for me to say anything.

139
00:07:59,176 --> 00:08:01,143
I can only keep watching.

140
00:08:01,345 --> 00:08:03,413
But an objective perspective...

141
00:08:03,714 --> 00:08:05,415
- might actually help.
- Right.

142
00:08:05,582 --> 00:08:08,284
Being slightly removed can help
you see things more clearly.

143
00:08:09,186 --> 00:08:11,487
Banu, are you here on a student visa?

144
00:08:11,855 --> 00:08:12,721
Yes.

145
00:08:13,057 --> 00:08:14,791
How often do you have to renew it?

146
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:16,993
I'm on a scholarship,

147
00:08:17,628 --> 00:08:21,197
so I got four years.

148
00:08:22,299 --> 00:08:23,599
What's your major again?

149
00:08:23,901 --> 00:08:24,868
AI.

150
00:08:25,135 --> 00:08:25,968
AI?

151
00:08:26,236 --> 00:08:28,070
Benjamin, you're in AI too, right?

152
00:08:28,071 --> 00:08:29,205
- Yes.
- I see.

153
00:08:29,206 --> 00:08:31,073
The software we made...

154
00:08:31,074 --> 00:08:32,675
was picked up by a bank.

155
00:08:33,143 --> 00:08:34,210
Really?

156
00:08:35,112 --> 00:08:36,679
These days, IT companies can't...

157
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:38,674
avoid AI.

158
00:08:38,948 --> 00:08:40,950
But why did you only study there briefly?

159
00:08:40,951 --> 00:08:42,852
Because of my parents' visa issues.

160
00:08:43,487 --> 00:08:46,756
They immigrated via an investment visa...

161
00:08:47,558 --> 00:08:50,560
and were supposed to get green cards,
but they didn't,

162
00:08:50,928 --> 00:08:52,428
so we kept going back and forth.

163
00:08:52,630 --> 00:08:54,864
I did some university there,
then came back to Korea.

164
00:08:55,331 --> 00:08:58,900
But in Guam
there's only one real university,

165
00:08:59,066 --> 00:09:01,334
it's called UOG,

166
00:09:01,905 --> 00:09:02,805
and it's...

167
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:04,640
Living there sounds cool.

168
00:09:04,641 --> 00:09:07,343
It's a tourist spot,
a place people go to vacation.

169
00:09:08,345 --> 00:09:10,212
I don't think he suspects me.
I think he knows.

170
00:09:10,781 --> 00:09:11,981
He couldn't look me in the eye.

171
00:09:12,417 --> 00:09:14,117
Like when you're about to catch
a chicken...

172
00:09:14,118 --> 00:09:15,718
and you can't look at it...

173
00:09:15,719 --> 00:09:16,586
because you feel bad.

174
00:09:18,922 --> 00:09:20,456
Are we having curry today?

175
00:09:20,524 --> 00:09:21,324
Should we?

176
00:09:21,825 --> 00:09:22,691
Let's do it.

177
00:09:23,127 --> 00:09:24,160
You don't eat pork, right?

178
00:09:24,428 --> 00:09:26,462
- Yes.
- There's pork, but...

179
00:09:26,664 --> 00:09:29,098
We can just grill the meat separately...

180
00:09:29,299 --> 00:09:30,900
and pour the curry over it.

181
00:09:31,101 --> 00:09:33,235
We can still have ours mixed that way.

182
00:09:33,404 --> 00:09:35,405
Good idea. Sugar, come help me.

183
00:09:39,510 --> 00:09:40,743
Smells great!

184
00:09:40,744 --> 00:09:42,178
Right? It looks great.

185
00:09:44,515 --> 00:09:46,015
I never thought I'd be making curry here.

186
00:09:47,084 --> 00:09:48,651
Do you think we can make it out alive?

187
00:09:49,153 --> 00:09:50,086
We can.

188
00:09:50,087 --> 00:09:52,021
Let's join forces.

189
00:09:52,423 --> 00:09:54,424
But let's enjoy this food first.

190
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:57,260
What do you think about Genie?

191
00:09:57,895 --> 00:09:58,695
Genie?

192
00:09:58,996 --> 00:10:00,330
- Yesterday,
- Oh, that?

193
00:10:00,331 --> 00:10:01,164
she didn't let go.

194
00:10:01,598 --> 00:10:02,765
It was selfish.

195
00:10:03,233 --> 00:10:04,633
From the beginning,

196
00:10:04,634 --> 00:10:06,836
I knew she had
zero intention of giving it up.

197
00:10:07,104 --> 00:10:08,738
But what I found strange was...

198
00:10:08,739 --> 00:10:11,507
It was strange...

199
00:10:11,508 --> 00:10:14,477
she pretended to run and cry.

200
00:10:14,878 --> 00:10:18,647
Fewer and fewer people care
if they get eliminated,

201
00:10:18,648 --> 00:10:20,883
and there are more people
who should be eliminated.

202
00:10:21,218 --> 00:10:22,151
That smells great.

203
00:10:22,152 --> 00:10:23,686
We should've cooked this earlier.

204
00:10:23,788 --> 00:10:24,921
Is it taking a while?

205
00:10:24,922 --> 00:10:25,989
I think so.

206
00:10:26,156 --> 00:10:27,957
- Maybe 5 or 10 more minutes
- That's not long!

207
00:10:31,095 --> 00:10:33,797
What's the hardest part about politics?

208
00:10:34,731 --> 00:10:36,165
Winning people's hearts, I guess.

209
00:10:36,166 --> 00:10:40,202
I've learned a lot this time too.

210
00:10:40,504 --> 00:10:43,039
What I think is necessary can be...

211
00:10:43,474 --> 00:10:45,842
very different from what the people want.

212
00:10:46,577 --> 00:10:51,114
The gap you feel from that mismatch can
be really difficult.

213
00:10:51,115 --> 00:10:54,350
So even good intentions can
sometimes look...

214
00:10:54,351 --> 00:10:56,152
like deception or violence to others.

215
00:10:57,588 --> 00:10:59,389
So winning people's hearts is...

216
00:10:59,390 --> 00:11:01,291
really difficult in the bigger picture.

217
00:11:01,925 --> 00:11:05,094
So even if you want to take power
with the best intentions,

218
00:11:05,095 --> 00:11:08,330
to take power,
you have to win over the public.

219
00:11:09,067 --> 00:11:10,867
So you end up using populism...

220
00:11:11,335 --> 00:11:13,369
and go against some of your own beliefs.

221
00:11:13,570 --> 00:11:15,271
And once that gets you elected,

222
00:11:15,272 --> 00:11:18,741
you can't just betray the people
who voted for you and do something else.

223
00:11:18,742 --> 00:11:22,845
If you don't deliver...

224
00:11:22,846 --> 00:11:24,213
what made people vote for you,

225
00:11:24,214 --> 00:11:26,215
then you'll be judged for it
during the next election.

226
00:11:26,216 --> 00:11:28,617
You'll end up a lame duck.

227
00:11:28,852 --> 00:11:31,253
The whole political system keeps...

228
00:11:31,254 --> 00:11:32,688
pushing both sides...

229
00:11:32,689 --> 00:11:34,590
toward more populism,

230
00:11:34,825 --> 00:11:36,959
and I think it's really dangerous.

231
00:11:37,561 --> 00:11:40,129
To win people's hearts,
it's hard to get elected...

232
00:11:40,130 --> 00:11:42,431
with just a vision and zero populism.

233
00:11:42,432 --> 00:11:43,499
It doesn't work.

234
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:45,035
I'm not normally someone...

235
00:11:45,036 --> 00:11:46,536
who follows current affairs that closely,

236
00:11:46,537 --> 00:11:47,870
but I've been thinking about it a lot.

237
00:11:47,871 --> 00:11:49,805
It's complicated and difficult.

238
00:11:50,374 --> 00:11:53,142
I think the problem is
that politicians don't debate enough.

239
00:11:53,610 --> 00:11:55,911
The country is split
right down the middle.

240
00:11:55,912 --> 00:11:57,146
If you go to certain regions,

241
00:11:57,147 --> 00:11:59,949
like somewhere in Gyeongsang-do,
my party is guaranteed to win.

242
00:11:59,950 --> 00:12:00,883
It's predetermined.

243
00:12:01,351 --> 00:12:03,452
They're not trying to win people's hearts.

244
00:12:03,654 --> 00:12:05,822
They're just trying to serve the party.

245
00:12:05,823 --> 00:12:07,790
- I see.
- Just running there guarantees election.

246
00:12:07,791 --> 00:12:09,859
- I see.
- So we live...

247
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:11,361
with our ears shut to all of that.

248
00:12:11,729 --> 00:12:14,697
I appear a lot on TV,

249
00:12:15,232 --> 00:12:17,733
but does what I'm saying
actually help politics?

250
00:12:19,236 --> 00:12:21,504
I'm basically spending
two hours on TV saying,

251
00:12:21,672 --> 00:12:24,440
"That guy's a bad guy."

252
00:12:25,342 --> 00:12:26,809
I keep wondering...

253
00:12:26,810 --> 00:12:28,577
if it's really the right thing to do.

254
00:12:29,313 --> 00:12:31,648
That's also partly...

255
00:12:31,649 --> 00:12:33,483
why I wasn't very interested.

256
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:35,317
Watching this debate made me wonder...

257
00:12:35,318 --> 00:12:37,686
why I hadn't tried to watch more of them.

258
00:12:37,687 --> 00:12:40,122
It was so interesting
and gave me a lot to think about.

259
00:12:40,624 --> 00:12:42,358
I wish there were more debates.

260
00:12:43,727 --> 00:12:44,694
Eat while it's hot.

261
00:12:44,695 --> 00:12:46,495
Who knows when we'll get to eat again?

262
00:12:46,496 --> 00:12:48,664
Exactly. We need to eat a lot today.

263
00:12:50,434 --> 00:12:51,367
It should taste fine.

264
00:12:53,270 --> 00:12:56,205
We don't have much time,
so I wanted to bring this up.

265
00:12:56,340 --> 00:12:59,009
Yesterday we briefly talked...

266
00:12:59,010 --> 00:13:00,977
about finding the top three residents
with the most money...

267
00:13:00,978 --> 00:13:04,213
and using that to identify the spy.

268
00:13:04,214 --> 00:13:06,015
We said we'd use the funds in that way.

269
00:13:06,016 --> 00:13:09,385
So I wondered if we could
make it a kind of moral line...

270
00:13:09,386 --> 00:13:12,121
and disclose how much we have.

271
00:13:12,923 --> 00:13:15,191
Could I argue against that?

272
00:13:15,192 --> 00:13:15,992
Go ahead.

273
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:18,695
When an idea like this comes up,

274
00:13:18,696 --> 00:13:21,364
it inevitably singles out certain people.

275
00:13:21,832 --> 00:13:24,633
But I'm not sure that's a way...

276
00:13:24,634 --> 00:13:25,801
to protect the vulnerable.

277
00:13:26,837 --> 00:13:28,838
The same goes for immunity.

278
00:13:29,106 --> 00:13:32,442
Using it or not is a personal choice.

279
00:13:32,443 --> 00:13:35,311
Treating people who hesitate to use it...

280
00:13:35,412 --> 00:13:38,447
like they're bad people feels...

281
00:13:38,448 --> 00:13:41,283
a lot like a witch hunt.

282
00:13:41,284 --> 00:13:43,152
It made me really uncomfortable,

283
00:13:43,153 --> 00:13:46,822
so I really empathized
with Genie yesterday.

284
00:13:46,823 --> 00:13:48,090
Dark Knight said yesterday...

285
00:13:48,091 --> 00:13:50,393
that Genie looked at the time as she ran.

286
00:13:50,394 --> 00:13:52,129
I couldn't agree with that part.

287
00:13:52,130 --> 00:13:54,764
Right. We can really feel assured...

288
00:13:54,891 --> 00:13:57,125
by your words, Gray.

289
00:13:57,868 --> 00:13:59,468
If you hadn't attacked Hippo.

290
00:14:02,359 --> 00:14:05,361
This isn't the time to joke around.

291
00:14:05,409 --> 00:14:08,211
Dark Knight, did you think I was acting?

292
00:14:08,212 --> 00:14:09,178
No.

293
00:14:18,422 --> 00:14:19,956
Remember, everyone,

294
00:14:20,357 --> 00:14:21,557
I always cooked for you.

295
00:14:21,858 --> 00:14:23,091
You're the best.

296
00:14:24,328 --> 00:14:26,629
But what does the spy really gain?

297
00:14:26,763 --> 00:14:27,663
What do you think?

298
00:14:28,097 --> 00:14:29,264
Well, I think,

299
00:14:30,100 --> 00:14:32,401
first, it's not getting caught
until the end.

300
00:14:32,402 --> 00:14:34,903
If they aren't caught,
maybe they get more prize money?

301
00:14:34,904 --> 00:14:38,340
There has to be a big incentive.

302
00:14:38,942 --> 00:14:40,743
Right, most likely.

303
00:14:40,877 --> 00:14:43,879
Today's game might be
about catching the spy.

304
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:44,947
Right?

305
00:14:44,948 --> 00:14:46,281
Figuring out who it is.

306
00:14:46,282 --> 00:14:48,351
That sounds fun and brutal.

307
00:14:48,352 --> 00:14:50,385
Everyone suspects everyone right now.

308
00:14:54,558 --> 00:14:55,858
(Tensions Under the Roof)

309
00:14:55,859 --> 00:14:58,227
(The Community)

310
00:15:02,550 --> 00:15:04,351
(Episode 10: Tonight, the Show is Mine)

311
00:15:04,434 --> 00:15:07,503
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

312
00:15:08,405 --> 00:15:10,939
Today's day and night activities...

313
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:12,775
may result in...

314
00:15:13,243 --> 00:15:14,143
up to...

315
00:15:15,812 --> 00:15:18,581
five evictions.

316
00:15:18,982 --> 00:15:19,882
Look at that.

317
00:15:21,185 --> 00:15:23,486
Good. The time has finally come.

318
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:25,888
It's the eighth day,
and there are still...

319
00:15:25,889 --> 00:15:27,723
eleven people left.

320
00:15:28,225 --> 00:15:30,793
From 1 to 3 to 5?

321
00:15:31,027 --> 00:15:33,795
Will anyone actually still be
here tomorrow?

322
00:15:34,698 --> 00:15:35,798
It's every man for himself.

323
00:15:36,701 --> 00:15:38,234
- Five people?
- First,

324
00:15:38,235 --> 00:15:40,603
I'll explain today's daytime mission.

325
00:15:40,604 --> 00:15:41,470
Five people?

326
00:15:41,805 --> 00:15:43,539
Today's daytime mission is...

327
00:15:44,141 --> 00:15:46,042
Life Speeches.

328
00:15:46,576 --> 00:15:48,443
- Life Speech?
- What?

329
00:15:48,444 --> 00:15:49,311
First,

330
00:15:49,846 --> 00:15:52,915
each resident chooses one keyword...

331
00:15:53,084 --> 00:15:56,253
and talks about their life
for 15 minutes...

332
00:15:56,254 --> 00:15:58,321
in any format they choose...

333
00:15:58,622 --> 00:16:01,556
in an effort to move the other residents.

334
00:16:01,557 --> 00:16:02,991
It's a popularity vote.

335
00:16:03,627 --> 00:16:04,527
Second,

336
00:16:04,961 --> 00:16:07,696
residents visit the Community Center...

337
00:16:07,697 --> 00:16:09,631
in the order decided by the leader...

338
00:16:09,866 --> 00:16:13,235
and choose one of three keywords.

339
00:16:14,037 --> 00:16:18,040
Each keyword can be chosen...

340
00:16:18,041 --> 00:16:19,775
by up to four people.

341
00:16:20,944 --> 00:16:25,548
Third, the speeches are given in the order
in which the keywords were chosen.

342
00:16:25,782 --> 00:16:27,750
After the speeches, residents vote...

343
00:16:28,018 --> 00:16:30,653
for the person they thought gave...

344
00:16:30,654 --> 00:16:32,488
the best speech...

345
00:16:32,489 --> 00:16:36,826
among the other two keywords.

346
00:16:37,327 --> 00:16:38,327
Fourth,

347
00:16:38,328 --> 00:16:41,764
the resident with the most votes...

348
00:16:41,765 --> 00:16:44,433
for each keyword receives
5,000 dollars in personal funds.

349
00:16:44,801 --> 00:16:46,935
The resident with the fewest votes will...

350
00:16:47,304 --> 00:16:48,838
be up for eviction.

351
00:16:50,073 --> 00:16:52,007
If there is a tie,
rankings will be determined...

352
00:16:52,243 --> 00:16:55,878
by their total favorability points.

353
00:16:56,146 --> 00:16:57,213
Fifth,

354
00:16:57,380 --> 00:17:00,949
if the leader is nominated for eviction
during the daytime mission,

355
00:17:01,384 --> 00:17:04,086
they are automatically exempted.

356
00:17:04,555 --> 00:17:07,790
In addition to having immunity,

357
00:17:08,124 --> 00:17:11,393
the leader may save
one of the other eviction candidates.

358
00:17:12,162 --> 00:17:14,897
The two residents not chosen...

359
00:17:15,532 --> 00:17:17,133
will be evicted.

360
00:17:18,268 --> 00:17:21,537
If they have immunity,

361
00:17:21,839 --> 00:17:25,608
it is used up, and they are not evicted.

362
00:17:26,175 --> 00:17:30,512
The Life Speeches will begin shortly.

363
00:17:31,114 --> 00:17:34,216
The leader must now visit
the Community Center...

364
00:17:34,617 --> 00:17:37,252
and decide the speech order.

365
00:17:37,654 --> 00:17:38,754
So many will...

366
00:17:39,289 --> 00:17:40,956
- be eliminated.
- Way too many.

367
00:17:42,759 --> 00:17:45,394
Can't I just do like I always do
and go choose first?

368
00:17:48,565 --> 00:17:49,798
Maybe not if it's important?

369
00:17:50,133 --> 00:17:52,501
Wait. We go in a decided order...

370
00:17:52,502 --> 00:17:54,436
and choose our keywords?

371
00:17:54,437 --> 00:17:57,005
Yes, but once a keyword is full,
you can't choose it anymore.

372
00:17:58,975 --> 00:18:02,177
Our leader will just have to use...

373
00:18:02,178 --> 00:18:04,413
- her best judgment...
- Yes.

374
00:18:04,714 --> 00:18:06,815
I'll figure it out.

375
00:18:08,685 --> 00:18:11,787
Is it better to go earlier or later?

376
00:18:15,025 --> 00:18:17,260
(Please choose
the keyword selection order.)

377
00:18:20,230 --> 00:18:24,533
(Speakers)

378
00:18:24,534 --> 00:18:25,634
(Superman)

379
00:18:25,635 --> 00:18:28,671
I've never really talked
about myself anywhere.

380
00:18:28,754 --> 00:18:31,088
I mean, Life Speeches are so...

381
00:18:31,341 --> 00:18:32,641
You shouldn't cry while doing them.

382
00:18:33,643 --> 00:18:34,710
I won't, anyway.

383
00:18:35,145 --> 00:18:36,779
This is so hard.

384
00:18:36,780 --> 00:18:39,081
- We're judging each other?
- Yes.

385
00:18:39,482 --> 00:18:40,549
What could the keywords be?

386
00:18:40,550 --> 00:18:41,917
What are we supposed to talk about?

387
00:18:41,918 --> 00:18:45,020
(Life Speeches)

388
00:18:45,021 --> 00:18:48,690
So, we're judging each other, right?

389
00:18:49,259 --> 00:18:51,560
The most suspicious members should be
eliminated first.

390
00:18:51,561 --> 00:18:53,963
- This is a good opportunity.
- Right.

391
00:18:53,964 --> 00:18:55,264
So someone needs...

392
00:18:55,289 --> 00:18:58,291
to find a way to let people know.

393
00:18:58,368 --> 00:19:00,236
We need to coordinate it well.

394
00:19:00,237 --> 00:19:01,203
Okay.

395
00:19:01,204 --> 00:19:02,037
(Voting attack plan)

396
00:19:02,472 --> 00:19:03,372
I had a feeling...

397
00:19:04,507 --> 00:19:05,907
I was going home today.

398
00:19:05,908 --> 00:19:06,842
Come on!

399
00:19:06,843 --> 00:19:08,243
You're good with words.

400
00:19:08,545 --> 00:19:11,113
I definitely said
that I wasn't good at speaking.

401
00:19:16,219 --> 00:19:17,152
I...

402
00:19:17,354 --> 00:19:21,457
I'm 99 percent sure
that Benjamin is the spy.

403
00:19:21,458 --> 00:19:22,258
Agreed.

404
00:19:22,259 --> 00:19:24,860
We have to hide
the fact that we're onto him...

405
00:19:25,228 --> 00:19:27,663
and choose the target for eviction
for the morning mission.

406
00:19:28,098 --> 00:19:32,001
I think everyone is
secretly going to choose Benjamin.

407
00:19:32,268 --> 00:19:33,168
Okay.

408
00:19:35,472 --> 00:19:36,338
Go Ae Shin.

409
00:19:36,873 --> 00:19:39,775
The important thing here is
that we vote...

410
00:19:39,776 --> 00:19:41,410
- and pick the person for last place.
- Right.

411
00:19:42,145 --> 00:19:44,813
- So I want us to all choose him.
- Same.

412
00:19:45,148 --> 00:19:48,150
First, I think we should boost
Benjamin's confidence as much as possible.

413
00:19:48,151 --> 00:19:49,384
I agree.

414
00:19:54,391 --> 00:19:56,425
(Speech order: Sugar)

415
00:19:56,426 --> 00:19:59,662
Clearly, it's better to go earlier
in this game.

416
00:19:59,663 --> 00:20:02,031
Because once a topic fills up,

417
00:20:02,032 --> 00:20:03,165
you can't choose it.

418
00:20:03,633 --> 00:20:06,468
Normally, when we have discussions,

419
00:20:06,469 --> 00:20:08,370
there are certain people who talk more...

420
00:20:08,371 --> 00:20:10,672
and those who talk less.

421
00:20:10,673 --> 00:20:13,775
So, considering those two groups...

422
00:20:13,776 --> 00:20:17,079
I let the quieter people go earlier...

423
00:20:17,080 --> 00:20:19,848
and put the more eloquent speakers later,

424
00:20:19,849 --> 00:20:24,419
as they could handle any topic easily.

425
00:20:24,754 --> 00:20:28,256
I thought the order could be
an important tool...

426
00:20:28,257 --> 00:20:30,759
to flush out the suspicious Benjamin.

427
00:20:30,760 --> 00:20:33,662
I decided I had to take advantage
of choosing the order...

428
00:20:33,797 --> 00:20:37,266
and place Benjamin at the end.

429
00:20:40,937 --> 00:20:43,172
(Keyword Selection Order: Sugar, Banu,
Go Ae Shin, Dark Knight,)

430
00:20:43,173 --> 00:20:45,240
(Michael, Superman, Gray, Genie,)

431
00:20:45,241 --> 00:20:47,609
(Ted, Benjamin, Polar Bear)

432
00:20:53,183 --> 00:20:55,851
The leader has finished
deciding the order.

433
00:20:56,352 --> 00:20:58,987
Sugar,

434
00:20:59,122 --> 00:21:03,592
please visit the Community Center
to choose your keyword.

435
00:21:05,061 --> 00:21:09,965
(Sugar, the first to choose)

436
00:21:09,966 --> 00:21:11,166
(Hypocrite)

437
00:21:11,167 --> 00:21:12,200
(Money)

438
00:21:12,201 --> 00:21:14,703
(Inferiority)

439
00:21:19,943 --> 00:21:21,778
I don't know what the keywords are,

440
00:21:21,779 --> 00:21:24,280
so I just chose myself to go last.

441
00:21:25,582 --> 00:21:27,716
I wonder what they'll be.

442
00:21:27,851 --> 00:21:30,486
Talking about myself is so uncomfortable.

443
00:21:30,754 --> 00:21:33,022
You have to lay yourself bare.

444
00:21:33,523 --> 00:21:35,891
Banu,

445
00:21:36,126 --> 00:21:38,160
please visit the Community Center...

446
00:21:38,161 --> 00:21:40,563
to choose your keyword.

447
00:21:41,197 --> 00:21:44,766
(The screen shows what's left.)

448
00:21:45,602 --> 00:21:46,535
Oh my gosh.

449
00:21:50,840 --> 00:21:52,707
(Money)

450
00:21:54,978 --> 00:21:59,281
(Hypocrite)

451
00:22:02,051 --> 00:22:03,618
(Money: 1)

452
00:22:03,619 --> 00:22:04,987
(Inferiority: 1)

453
00:22:06,956 --> 00:22:08,557
The speech itself is important,

454
00:22:08,558 --> 00:22:11,493
but the relationships we've fought
to build are really important too.

455
00:22:11,494 --> 00:22:14,230
- You could see it that way.
- Because...

456
00:22:14,231 --> 00:22:16,698
you're more emotionally involved,
and the judging criteria broaden.

457
00:22:16,766 --> 00:22:17,733
True.

458
00:22:17,734 --> 00:22:19,835
So it doesn't really matter
how much of a skilled orator you are.

459
00:22:20,403 --> 00:22:22,938
Either way,
someone has to be eliminated by vote.

460
00:22:23,473 --> 00:22:27,009
So what will you do
regarding the person you suspect?

461
00:22:27,010 --> 00:22:29,178
Will you go off that or judge it honestly?

462
00:22:29,179 --> 00:22:30,546
When voting?

463
00:22:30,547 --> 00:22:32,215
The votes can be used
as a way to attack this time.

464
00:22:32,216 --> 00:22:33,248
Oh, right.

465
00:22:35,752 --> 00:22:36,785
This is tough.

466
00:22:39,923 --> 00:22:40,990
Hypocrite?

467
00:22:44,494 --> 00:22:46,628
(Money)

468
00:22:48,164 --> 00:22:49,531
So this is how it goes.

469
00:22:51,367 --> 00:22:54,636
(Hypocrite: 1, Money: 4, Inferiority: 3)

470
00:23:18,261 --> 00:23:21,296
(Hypocrite)

471
00:23:23,499 --> 00:23:26,167
(Speeches)

472
00:23:27,337 --> 00:23:29,305
We can get him. It's a bit off,

473
00:23:29,306 --> 00:23:32,641
- but I think it has a chance of working.
- What do you mean?

474
00:23:32,808 --> 00:23:35,844
Benjamin,

475
00:23:36,212 --> 00:23:38,346
please visit the Community Center...

476
00:23:38,347 --> 00:23:40,682
to choose your keyword.

477
00:23:45,355 --> 00:23:47,323
It's a great day to die.

478
00:23:48,792 --> 00:23:50,827
First, I have to survive
to be able to act.

479
00:23:50,828 --> 00:23:54,096
I need to try to improve my chances
of surviving by even one percent.

480
00:23:54,664 --> 00:23:56,665
(Hypocrite: 2, Money: 4, Inferiority: 3)

481
00:24:06,175 --> 00:24:07,175
I think we can do it.

482
00:24:07,176 --> 00:24:09,110
It's a little different
than I expected, though.

483
00:24:09,111 --> 00:24:11,646
The leader can
take advantage of their immunity.

484
00:24:11,647 --> 00:24:12,580
Three people get picked.

485
00:24:12,581 --> 00:24:14,183
One of them gets saved,

486
00:24:14,184 --> 00:24:15,550
the leader survives,
and another is eliminated.

487
00:24:16,052 --> 00:24:16,918
One person...

488
00:24:17,887 --> 00:24:18,753
One person...

489
00:24:19,956 --> 00:24:20,856
One person...

490
00:24:22,191 --> 00:24:23,124
One person...

491
00:24:26,996 --> 00:24:30,332
(Inferiority)

492
00:24:30,867 --> 00:24:32,334
Actually, I mentioned that...

493
00:24:33,269 --> 00:24:34,202
He's out, guys.

494
00:24:34,203 --> 00:24:35,303
Benjamin is coming back.

495
00:24:36,607 --> 00:24:39,375
He's here. Let's talk about it later.

496
00:24:44,547 --> 00:24:46,916
So there are a lot of things
that are confusing.

497
00:24:46,917 --> 00:24:49,084
I'm not sure what actually raises my odds.

498
00:24:55,591 --> 00:24:56,457
What did you choose?

499
00:24:57,427 --> 00:24:58,293
Inferiority.

500
00:24:59,429 --> 00:25:00,462
- Really?
- Inferiority?

501
00:25:00,630 --> 00:25:01,863
That's surprising.

502
00:25:02,131 --> 00:25:04,733
(Hypocrite: 2, Money: 4, Inferiority: 4)

503
00:25:06,869 --> 00:25:10,572
(Hypocrite)

504
00:25:10,740 --> 00:25:12,677
(Hypocrite: Go Ae Shin, Ted, Polar Bear)

505
00:25:12,678 --> 00:25:14,941
(Money: Banu, Michael, Gray, Genie)

506
00:25:14,942 --> 00:25:16,778
(Inferiority: Sugar, Dark Knight,
Superman, Benjamin)

507
00:25:16,779 --> 00:25:19,614
All keywords have been selected.

508
00:25:20,516 --> 00:25:22,684
The speech order will be by keyword:

509
00:25:23,018 --> 00:25:28,423
Hypocrite, Money, Inferiority,
in that order.

510
00:25:28,992 --> 00:25:32,428
The speech order is the same
as the keyword selection order,

511
00:25:32,695 --> 00:25:35,463
and the format and content are up
to your discretion.

512
00:25:36,165 --> 00:25:39,901
Residents, for the next hour,

513
00:25:40,036 --> 00:25:43,338
please return to your bedrooms
to prepare your speeches.

514
00:25:55,184 --> 00:25:57,318
Hello, I am a hypocrite...

515
00:26:03,793 --> 00:26:06,695
This was your lump of inferiority, Sugar!

516
00:26:23,913 --> 00:26:25,413
Okay, I'll just try...

517
00:26:26,082 --> 00:26:29,151
freestyling it.

518
00:26:29,652 --> 00:26:31,452
(One hour later)

519
00:26:31,453 --> 00:26:32,921
I think I might be mentally ill.

520
00:26:33,490 --> 00:26:34,723
I felt a wave of sadness...

521
00:26:34,724 --> 00:26:36,625
and screamed out
that I wanted to eat Peking duck.

522
00:26:37,060 --> 00:26:38,527
People still tease me about it.

523
00:26:38,528 --> 00:26:40,596
She cried because she couldn't eat duck?

524
00:26:40,930 --> 00:26:43,098
How can something like that make you cry?

525
00:26:43,099 --> 00:26:45,901
You're all playing in the upper class now.

526
00:26:47,103 --> 00:26:48,370
The Benjamin Show is over.

527
00:26:49,205 --> 00:26:50,438
I'm very good at Korean.

528
00:26:50,439 --> 00:26:52,174
I don't think I'll need English anymore.

529
00:26:58,781 --> 00:27:01,015
So we have to go in there
and do it alone...

530
00:27:01,558 --> 00:27:04,193
just like last time.

531
00:27:05,521 --> 00:27:07,055
Wow, it's so cool.

532
00:27:07,757 --> 00:27:08,623
There's something...

533
00:27:09,992 --> 00:27:12,327
The vibe here is really weird.

534
00:27:13,930 --> 00:27:14,963
I'm nervous.

535
00:27:15,431 --> 00:27:17,899
Usually when things change like this,

536
00:27:18,034 --> 00:27:19,701
I get more comfortable,

537
00:27:19,702 --> 00:27:21,737
but today I just feel more nervous.

538
00:27:26,876 --> 00:27:28,276
I'm really nervous.

539
00:27:28,544 --> 00:27:30,645
Really? I've never seen you nervous.

540
00:27:30,646 --> 00:27:32,680
Me too. What gives?

541
00:27:46,829 --> 00:27:50,398
We'll now begin the Life Speeches.

542
00:27:51,367 --> 00:27:55,103
The first keyword is Hypocrite.

543
00:27:56,172 --> 00:27:59,307
Go Ae Shin.

544
00:27:59,308 --> 00:28:02,310
Please enter the chat room.

545
00:28:02,311 --> 00:28:03,345
- You got this!
- Gosh.

546
00:28:03,346 --> 00:28:04,879
Good luck!

547
00:28:04,880 --> 00:28:06,916
- You can do it!
- You got this!

548
00:28:06,917 --> 00:28:08,016
(Hypocrite: Go Ae Shin, Ted, Polar Bear)

549
00:28:08,017 --> 00:28:08,917
I'm screwed!

550
00:28:16,726 --> 00:28:17,526
Can you hear me?

551
00:28:18,961 --> 00:28:20,194
Maybe she can't hear us?

552
00:28:22,298 --> 00:28:24,899
Please begin your speech.

553
00:28:28,337 --> 00:28:30,138
(Life Speech: Hypocrite, Go Ae Shin)

554
00:28:30,139 --> 00:28:32,240
I've only ever come in here to cook,

555
00:28:32,265 --> 00:28:35,167
but coming in here to give a speech is
nerve-wracking.

556
00:28:35,578 --> 00:28:37,045
Anyway,

557
00:28:37,513 --> 00:28:40,515
I thought about when I was a hypocrite...

558
00:28:40,516 --> 00:28:41,649
most recently.

559
00:28:42,084 --> 00:28:44,586
When I thought about it,

560
00:28:44,587 --> 00:28:48,322
I realized my hypocrisy started...

561
00:28:48,323 --> 00:28:49,624
when I joined this community.

562
00:28:49,625 --> 00:28:52,093
I used to say my life's keywords were...

563
00:28:52,094 --> 00:28:54,528
loyalty, justice, and promises,

564
00:28:54,529 --> 00:28:56,330
but after coming here,

565
00:28:56,799 --> 00:28:59,734
when I watched Gray,

566
00:28:59,735 --> 00:29:01,403
I saw someone who could make
a firm decision...

567
00:29:01,404 --> 00:29:02,537
when he killed someone.

568
00:29:02,905 --> 00:29:07,075
I actually exchanged codes...

569
00:29:07,076 --> 00:29:08,810
with Benjamin, who's sitting over there.

570
00:29:09,178 --> 00:29:11,579
We had an easy score, only four points.

571
00:29:11,815 --> 00:29:14,449
I knew all of Benjamin's codes,

572
00:29:15,117 --> 00:29:16,718
so I'd be lying
if I said I didn't consider it.

573
00:29:17,653 --> 00:29:18,853
"Should I kill Benjamin?"

574
00:29:20,590 --> 00:29:22,424
I thought about it,

575
00:29:22,992 --> 00:29:23,925
but I didn't!

576
00:29:26,063 --> 00:29:28,330
When I pictured Benjamin's face,

577
00:29:28,731 --> 00:29:30,198
I felt like I shouldn't.

578
00:29:31,100 --> 00:29:33,301
But when I heard that Gray attacked...

579
00:29:33,769 --> 00:29:36,537
someone else,
when he talked about it like that,

580
00:29:37,306 --> 00:29:39,641
I thought, "I should've killed Benjamin."

581
00:29:40,710 --> 00:29:41,877
Sorry, Benjamin!

582
00:29:42,078 --> 00:29:43,845
I really did think that.

583
00:29:44,180 --> 00:29:46,181
You said a keyword
for your life was loyalty.

584
00:29:46,849 --> 00:29:47,983
Is it really?

585
00:29:50,119 --> 00:29:52,887
I think there were many other choices
I could have made.

586
00:29:52,888 --> 00:29:55,890
But the reason I didn't make them was...

587
00:29:56,158 --> 00:29:58,960
because I wondered
what the people watching...

588
00:29:58,961 --> 00:30:00,228
would think of me.

589
00:30:00,763 --> 00:30:02,297
"If this weren't being broadcast,"

590
00:30:02,298 --> 00:30:04,833
"would I have made the same choices?"

591
00:30:05,401 --> 00:30:08,102
When I think about it, I don't think
I would have acted the same way at all.

592
00:30:08,204 --> 00:30:11,873
So one of my hypocritical behaviors is...

593
00:30:12,141 --> 00:30:14,809
the hypocrisy I show
in front of the cameras.

594
00:30:14,810 --> 00:30:17,112
I think that counts
as hypocritical behavior too.

595
00:30:17,714 --> 00:30:19,548
Anyway, everyone, let's use...

596
00:30:20,048 --> 00:30:23,151
this contradictory phrase,
"good hypocrisy,"

597
00:30:23,853 --> 00:30:25,787
and leave...

598
00:30:26,315 --> 00:30:27,915
this place happy. Thank you.

599
00:30:29,892 --> 00:30:32,227
And going first is the hardest.

600
00:30:36,465 --> 00:30:39,100
Please begin your speech.

601
00:30:39,101 --> 00:30:41,569
(Life Speech: Hypocrite, Ted)

602
00:30:45,508 --> 00:30:48,810
Of Hypocrite, Money and Inferiority,

603
00:30:49,312 --> 00:30:51,914
I agonized most over Hypocrite.

604
00:30:52,782 --> 00:30:55,450
Because, for me,

605
00:30:56,552 --> 00:30:58,419
it's a dream...

606
00:30:59,021 --> 00:31:00,655
I don't usually tell people about.

607
00:31:01,390 --> 00:31:02,824
We might interpret that word differently.

608
00:31:03,359 --> 00:31:06,361
I've been seen as a nice person.

609
00:31:06,362 --> 00:31:08,797
It's the image I'm known for.

610
00:31:09,199 --> 00:31:12,334
But I don't want praise for being nice.

611
00:31:13,201 --> 00:31:15,437
I had an unkind side to me,

612
00:31:15,438 --> 00:31:18,373
but I suppressed it and pretended
to be nice all the way to the end.

613
00:31:18,374 --> 00:31:19,941
That is what I want credit for.

614
00:31:20,577 --> 00:31:23,111
Let me explain what I mean...

615
00:31:23,312 --> 00:31:25,813
and how my dream is related
to this keyword, Hypocrite.

616
00:31:25,948 --> 00:31:27,248
I will tell you a story
from a long time ago.

617
00:31:28,551 --> 00:31:30,384
Very few people in my life...

618
00:31:30,986 --> 00:31:34,555
have ever heard this story.

619
00:31:35,791 --> 00:31:37,858
Even among my acquaintances,

620
00:31:37,859 --> 00:31:38,993
maybe only around five have heard it.

621
00:31:39,362 --> 00:31:42,830
I really don't tell this story
because it's so personal.

622
00:31:43,299 --> 00:31:44,899
I've only told certain friends...

623
00:31:45,134 --> 00:31:47,335
because I saw
a similar mental danger in them,

624
00:31:47,336 --> 00:31:48,937
just to reassure them...

625
00:31:49,238 --> 00:31:50,939
that they weren't alone.

626
00:31:50,940 --> 00:31:53,241
Other than doing that once or twice,

627
00:31:53,242 --> 00:31:55,043
I never really bring it up.

628
00:31:55,445 --> 00:31:58,913
But now it's an old story,

629
00:32:00,283 --> 00:32:03,418
so I thought it might be okay to tell it.

630
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:05,387
Back in college,

631
00:32:06,222 --> 00:32:08,390
I was walking slowly to class.

632
00:32:08,891 --> 00:32:11,292
About 50 meters ahead of me,
I saw a very close friend.

633
00:32:11,861 --> 00:32:13,829
Normally, I would have called out...

634
00:32:13,830 --> 00:32:15,197
or run over to catch up.

635
00:32:15,865 --> 00:32:17,465
The moment I realized, "That's my friend,"

636
00:32:17,466 --> 00:32:20,034
I stopped walking.
I just thought, "Oh, it's him."

637
00:32:20,970 --> 00:32:22,003
And I stood still.

638
00:32:23,506 --> 00:32:25,240
I waited until he walked
all the way to the end...

639
00:32:25,641 --> 00:32:27,275
and disappeared out of sight.

640
00:32:28,578 --> 00:32:30,178
Only after he was out of sight,

641
00:32:30,613 --> 00:32:31,713
did I start walking again.

642
00:32:32,815 --> 00:32:33,715
Because...

643
00:32:34,150 --> 00:32:35,717
if we ran into each other,
I would have to say hello.

644
00:32:36,719 --> 00:32:38,153
I'd have to talk to him.

645
00:32:39,488 --> 00:32:40,479
And this is another day on campus.

646
00:32:41,223 --> 00:32:44,025
Our dorm was a six-person room,

647
00:32:44,393 --> 00:32:45,827
and you could see through a window...

648
00:32:45,828 --> 00:32:47,195
if the light inside was on.

649
00:32:47,696 --> 00:32:48,562
I remember...

650
00:32:49,365 --> 00:32:50,665
going up the stairs,

651
00:32:51,400 --> 00:32:54,802
and when I saw the light on
above our dorm door,

652
00:32:55,538 --> 00:32:58,473
I actually cursed under my breath,
which I don't even do now.

653
00:32:58,874 --> 00:32:59,807
"I deliberately..."

654
00:33:00,143 --> 00:33:02,143
"stayed out late and came back..."

655
00:33:02,144 --> 00:33:04,178
"at a time when everyone
should have been asleep,"

656
00:33:04,179 --> 00:33:05,580
"even when I was exhausted."

657
00:33:05,581 --> 00:33:07,436
"But why is the light on?
Why is someone awake?"

658
00:33:08,117 --> 00:33:09,751
It meant I would have to walk in
and talk to someone.

659
00:33:11,187 --> 00:33:14,422
I loved stepping into a pitch-black room
when no one was there.

660
00:33:14,423 --> 00:33:16,625
That silence was pure peace. I loved it.

661
00:33:18,461 --> 00:33:20,195
I really related to that.

662
00:33:20,396 --> 00:33:22,997
I also try to be a good person,

663
00:33:22,998 --> 00:33:24,933
and I push myself really hard.

664
00:33:24,934 --> 00:33:29,637
I strain to stay polite and friendly,
so I completely understood.

665
00:33:30,639 --> 00:33:33,641
I used to dread dealing with people.

666
00:33:34,142 --> 00:33:35,043
It exhausted me.

667
00:33:35,845 --> 00:33:37,778
I even wondered if I had social anxiety.

668
00:33:37,779 --> 00:33:40,181
I felt genuine panic
whenever someone I knew came up to me.

669
00:33:41,049 --> 00:33:43,885
There was one time
when I felt things had gone too far.

670
00:33:44,186 --> 00:33:46,787
I was pulling an all-nighter
studying in the cafeteria.

671
00:33:47,424 --> 00:33:48,957
I was cramming hard
because the next morning...

672
00:33:48,958 --> 00:33:50,592
I had an eight or nine o'clock exam.

673
00:33:50,793 --> 00:33:52,460
I was cramming like crazy...

674
00:33:53,028 --> 00:33:55,096
when a younger student sat down
right across from me.

675
00:33:56,165 --> 00:33:58,466
He said,
"Could I ask you for some advice?"

676
00:33:59,668 --> 00:34:01,369
I worried he might feel awkward...

677
00:34:01,370 --> 00:34:02,803
if he noticed I was studying,

678
00:34:03,072 --> 00:34:05,040
so I looked up and said,
"Sure, have a seat,"

679
00:34:05,041 --> 00:34:07,609
and quickly cleared my desk...

680
00:34:08,043 --> 00:34:09,643
afraid he might realize
I had a test coming up.

681
00:34:09,746 --> 00:34:11,212
"You weren't busy, were you?"

682
00:34:11,213 --> 00:34:12,880
"Not at all. I was just taking a break."

683
00:34:13,949 --> 00:34:15,716
When I saw myself saying all that,

684
00:34:15,717 --> 00:34:17,318
I thought, "I might have a problem."

685
00:34:17,753 --> 00:34:19,320
"This is dangerous."

686
00:34:20,456 --> 00:34:21,389
I kind of realized it then.

687
00:34:21,390 --> 00:34:23,491
And when I looked back,

688
00:34:24,026 --> 00:34:27,261
I realized
it was classic "good child syndrome."

689
00:34:28,197 --> 00:34:31,466
If anyone looked objectively
at how I grew up,

690
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:33,167
they would probably say,

691
00:34:33,435 --> 00:34:36,437
"With an upbringing like that,
there is no way you could go wrong."

692
00:34:37,139 --> 00:34:39,574
"Complaining that you turned out bad..."

693
00:34:39,808 --> 00:34:41,108
"is ridiculous."

694
00:34:41,577 --> 00:34:43,978
"You ought to be a good person."

695
00:34:43,979 --> 00:34:46,080
"What could you possibly be
unhappy about?"

696
00:34:46,482 --> 00:34:48,516
My family was not wealthy,

697
00:34:48,517 --> 00:34:50,952
but emotionally, I grew up in...

698
00:34:50,953 --> 00:34:52,320
a stable environment.

699
00:34:53,055 --> 00:34:54,455
So at some point...

700
00:34:54,657 --> 00:34:56,458
it wasn't about what I liked anymore.

701
00:34:56,459 --> 00:34:59,761
I realized,
"I have to choose what I dislike."

702
00:35:00,395 --> 00:35:02,163
"That was the price
of a sheltered childhood."

703
00:35:02,932 --> 00:35:04,966
I struggled with this
throughout my twenties.

704
00:35:05,568 --> 00:35:07,702
I even tried to break the habit.
"I'm not doing this anymore."

705
00:35:07,836 --> 00:35:08,936
I tried for one whole month...

706
00:35:09,505 --> 00:35:11,072
I decided to let my darker impulses
take over...

707
00:35:11,073 --> 00:35:13,073
and lived exactly on my own terms.

708
00:35:14,710 --> 00:35:15,843
After that month, I realized.

709
00:35:16,779 --> 00:35:19,146
Doing whatever I wanted
made me uncomfortable.

710
00:35:19,882 --> 00:35:21,149
Before that,

711
00:35:21,383 --> 00:35:23,351
I assumed I acted nice...

712
00:35:23,786 --> 00:35:26,788
because I felt
people were forcing me to be.

713
00:35:26,789 --> 00:35:27,989
Once I let those impulses run free,

714
00:35:27,990 --> 00:35:30,392
I found out that I had grown
into someone...

715
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:32,794
who preferred being able to control it.

716
00:35:32,795 --> 00:35:34,996
I actually preferred being
that kind of person.

717
00:35:35,631 --> 00:35:37,365
That's the life of a hypocrite I dream of.

718
00:35:37,499 --> 00:35:38,599
Going forward,

719
00:35:39,435 --> 00:35:41,770
I want to take pride in...

720
00:35:41,771 --> 00:35:44,205
my ability to hold myself back.

721
00:35:44,607 --> 00:35:46,341
Not because I was born virtuous,

722
00:35:46,742 --> 00:35:49,143
but because I choose to live
as an earnest,

723
00:35:49,345 --> 00:35:52,414
deliberate, and admirable hypocrite.

724
00:35:52,415 --> 00:35:54,516
It was an honor to share this dream
with all of you.

725
00:35:54,517 --> 00:35:56,618
I'll live as a hypocrite to the very end.

726
00:35:56,619 --> 00:35:57,619
Thank you.

727
00:36:01,156 --> 00:36:04,358
That was the moment
I truly came to respect Ted.

728
00:36:04,927 --> 00:36:07,762
Hearing about his internal struggle...

729
00:36:07,763 --> 00:36:11,132
and his deliberate effort
to keep himself in check,

730
00:36:11,566 --> 00:36:13,501
made everything click.

731
00:36:13,502 --> 00:36:15,770
I thought,
"Ted genuinely is a good person."

732
00:36:15,771 --> 00:36:17,939
He is not just faking kindness.
It is who he chooses to be.

733
00:36:19,041 --> 00:36:22,543
The topic "Hypocrite"
really caught me off guard.

734
00:36:22,544 --> 00:36:24,129
(Life Speech: Hypocrite, Polar Bear)

735
00:36:24,246 --> 00:36:26,581
Since I joined this Community,

736
00:36:26,582 --> 00:36:30,985
it's probably the word I've thought
about more than any other time in my life.

737
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:34,589
It made me want to ask all of you.

738
00:36:34,957 --> 00:36:37,711
"Is being a hypocrite necessarily
a bad thing?"

739
00:36:38,661 --> 00:36:40,095
Well, here's what I think.

740
00:36:40,096 --> 00:36:41,594
Whenever instincts clash with reason,

741
00:36:41,594 --> 00:36:44,466
or desires collide with principles,

742
00:36:44,467 --> 00:36:47,202
hypocrisy naturally creeps in.

743
00:36:47,203 --> 00:36:49,537
But at the end of the day,

744
00:36:50,205 --> 00:36:52,706
you know which choice is better.

745
00:36:53,409 --> 00:36:56,478
Our impulses and our selfish desires
urge us one way,

746
00:36:56,779 --> 00:36:59,948
but deep down, we instinctively sense...

747
00:37:00,115 --> 00:37:03,050
the right path forward.

748
00:37:03,619 --> 00:37:06,330
In the end, that hypocritical effort...

749
00:37:06,331 --> 00:37:09,257
might just be proof that we are capable
of becoming...

750
00:37:09,625 --> 00:37:12,460
better people.

751
00:37:21,603 --> 00:37:22,770
I was so bored.

752
00:37:23,038 --> 00:37:24,739
It felt totally pointless.

753
00:37:25,240 --> 00:37:27,942
In the military, we have "moral training."

754
00:37:28,310 --> 00:37:30,210
They show you videos...

755
00:37:30,412 --> 00:37:32,313
trying to teach moral lessons,

756
00:37:32,314 --> 00:37:34,351
but none of it really gets through to me.

757
00:37:34,352 --> 00:37:35,883
I'm a total realist.

758
00:37:35,884 --> 00:37:38,586
It's the same in the real world.

759
00:37:38,587 --> 00:37:40,922
They're more focused on things like...

760
00:37:40,923 --> 00:37:43,491
trends and documents up at the top.

761
00:37:43,492 --> 00:37:45,259
They calculate things from up there,

762
00:37:45,260 --> 00:37:46,794
and their math is completely different
from mine.

763
00:37:48,197 --> 00:37:50,632
The Life Speech for the next keyword...

764
00:37:50,633 --> 00:37:52,801
will begin in ten minutes.

765
00:37:53,002 --> 00:37:57,004
Residents, please take a break.

766
00:37:57,873 --> 00:37:58,840
We did it!

767
00:37:58,841 --> 00:38:00,842
- I'm glad it's over.
- The speech stress is finally gone.

768
00:38:00,843 --> 00:38:02,477
I can finally relax.

769
00:38:05,080 --> 00:38:06,347
It feels great to be done early.

770
00:38:07,116 --> 00:38:08,383
We're all done.

771
00:38:10,753 --> 00:38:12,454
It's such a relief.

772
00:38:13,883 --> 00:38:15,722
Looks like Benjamin's wheels are turning,
right?

773
00:38:16,258 --> 00:38:17,124
Yes.

774
00:38:19,161 --> 00:38:21,662
Sorry, but honestly,
I wasn't even listening.

775
00:38:22,397 --> 00:38:25,532
I was too wrapped up in my own speech.

776
00:38:28,771 --> 00:38:33,641
The second round of Life Speeches
will begin shortly.

777
00:38:34,743 --> 00:38:36,077
She's going up without notes?

778
00:38:36,879 --> 00:38:39,581
Since I chose the topic "Money,"

779
00:38:39,982 --> 00:38:41,749
I'm about to confess something.

780
00:38:41,750 --> 00:38:43,117
I was born into...

781
00:38:43,453 --> 00:38:45,220
a financially well-off family.

782
00:38:45,655 --> 00:38:46,554
However...

783
00:38:47,055 --> 00:38:48,889
- Must be nice.
- I'm jealous.

784
00:38:48,890 --> 00:38:51,092
I'm not an heiress or anything.

785
00:38:51,093 --> 00:38:54,662
My dad was just
a very successful businessman.

786
00:38:54,797 --> 00:38:59,067
Growing up, I got whatever I wanted,

787
00:38:59,401 --> 00:39:02,035
never even cleaned my own room,

788
00:39:02,036 --> 00:39:05,840
and never gave money a second thought.

789
00:39:06,608 --> 00:39:09,577
I honestly never worried about money.

790
00:39:09,578 --> 00:39:11,278
Not even once.

791
00:39:12,381 --> 00:39:14,915
When I hear that, all I feel is envy.

792
00:39:15,117 --> 00:39:16,317
I'm so jealous.

793
00:39:16,318 --> 00:39:19,053
She said she never had to worry
about money growing up.

794
00:39:19,155 --> 00:39:20,988
Never had to clean...

795
00:39:20,989 --> 00:39:22,657
or cook.

796
00:39:22,658 --> 00:39:24,292
Is that kind of life really possible?

797
00:39:24,526 --> 00:39:27,228
That's the kind of life
I always dreamed of...

798
00:39:27,563 --> 00:39:31,165
Then I found something
I actually wanted to do with my life.

799
00:39:31,733 --> 00:39:33,333
Study abroad.

800
00:39:33,334 --> 00:39:36,203
I wanted to go overseas for school...

801
00:39:36,573 --> 00:39:38,840
and my parents supported me.

802
00:39:39,074 --> 00:39:41,709
They covered the costs,
and I came to Korea.

803
00:39:41,977 --> 00:39:44,378
At first,
I planned to stay for just six months...

804
00:39:44,613 --> 00:39:46,581
and the money I spent...

805
00:39:47,049 --> 00:39:48,483
- How much did she spend?
- In a month,

806
00:39:49,685 --> 00:39:53,688
I went through 3,000 or 4,000 dollars
just on living expenses.

807
00:39:55,624 --> 00:39:56,490
Then...

808
00:39:57,392 --> 00:39:59,893
six months later,
the situation back in Iran...

809
00:39:59,894 --> 00:40:01,795
took a terrible turn.

810
00:40:01,796 --> 00:40:03,564
The currency crashed,

811
00:40:03,565 --> 00:40:05,165
and the exchange rate dropped by 30 times.

812
00:40:07,469 --> 00:40:08,369
So...

813
00:40:08,871 --> 00:40:11,906
my dad's business at the time...

814
00:40:11,907 --> 00:40:13,308
went under as well.

815
00:40:13,309 --> 00:40:14,375
Really?

816
00:40:14,576 --> 00:40:17,745
Suddenly, everything turned upside down.

817
00:40:18,513 --> 00:40:20,647
I thought, "This is it. Time to step up,"

818
00:40:20,817 --> 00:40:23,485
and started looking for a part-time job.

819
00:40:25,266 --> 00:40:26,821
Picture this.

820
00:40:27,222 --> 00:40:29,089
I'd never worked a day in my life,

821
00:40:29,090 --> 00:40:31,158
and suddenly I was working
at a chicken joint.

822
00:40:31,760 --> 00:40:34,395
It was really grueling.

823
00:40:34,396 --> 00:40:36,797
When I first arrived in Korea,
I had some baby fat on me.

824
00:40:36,798 --> 00:40:38,733
I weighed almost 60 kg.

825
00:40:38,869 --> 00:40:41,936
Between skipping meals and working,
the weight just melted off.

826
00:40:41,937 --> 00:40:44,105
That was when I realized...

827
00:40:44,106 --> 00:40:48,076
spending money you earned yourself
hits completely different.

828
00:40:48,644 --> 00:40:50,377
I had never understood that before.

829
00:40:51,380 --> 00:40:53,881
That very first paycheck
from my part-time job...

830
00:40:54,717 --> 00:40:57,117
meant the world to me.

831
00:40:57,719 --> 00:40:58,986
Because it was so hard-earned...

832
00:40:58,987 --> 00:41:01,455
I tried so hard to budget carefully.

833
00:41:02,258 --> 00:41:05,626
I saved up, paid my own tuition,

834
00:41:06,128 --> 00:41:10,397
finished language school,
landed scholarships.

835
00:41:11,099 --> 00:41:15,236
So when people tell me,
"Must be nice being born rich,"

836
00:41:15,337 --> 00:41:18,439
or say, "I wish we could trade lives,"

837
00:41:18,573 --> 00:41:21,975
I think they're looking at it
the wrong way.

838
00:41:22,612 --> 00:41:25,813
Being born into wealth doesn't
make you happy.

839
00:41:26,448 --> 00:41:29,483
Some people use wealth to do
terrible things,

840
00:41:29,484 --> 00:41:32,586
and plenty of wealthy people
struggle with severe depression.

841
00:41:32,587 --> 00:41:34,588
Money can't buy happiness.

842
00:41:34,890 --> 00:41:38,125
If you shift your perspective,
you can always find a way forward.

843
00:41:38,660 --> 00:41:41,061
That's the message I wanted to share.

844
00:41:41,396 --> 00:41:44,132
Thank you for listening.

845
00:41:49,838 --> 00:41:51,338
Whenever you're ready.

846
00:41:51,673 --> 00:41:55,109
I'm about to bring up
another uncomfortable truth.

847
00:41:56,511 --> 00:41:58,712
Rappers and money go hand in hand.

848
00:41:59,414 --> 00:42:01,415
The two are practically inseparable.

849
00:42:01,616 --> 00:42:04,518
In hip-hop, flexing is taken for granted.

850
00:42:04,653 --> 00:42:06,587
A culture built around showing off...

851
00:42:06,588 --> 00:42:08,923
ends up driving so many rappers broke.

852
00:42:09,257 --> 00:42:11,225
I feel like the Korean rap scene...

853
00:42:11,226 --> 00:42:13,293
is heading down the wrong path.

854
00:42:13,294 --> 00:42:15,495
With that in mind,
I'm going to rap for you.

855
00:42:18,767 --> 00:42:21,435
(Freestyle Rap: "Spy")

856
00:42:22,204 --> 00:42:25,572
<i>Undercover spy, I'm on your tail</i>

857
00:42:27,242 --> 00:42:29,443
<i>Get you in my sight, it's KI double L</i>

858
00:42:30,345 --> 00:42:33,981
<i>You break the unity of our Community.</i>

859
00:42:38,187 --> 00:42:39,953
My freestyle...

860
00:42:39,954 --> 00:42:41,055
was an absolute train wreck.

861
00:42:41,056 --> 00:42:43,757
All I did was rap about taking
down the spy.

862
00:42:43,758 --> 00:42:46,227
Just lines like "I'll freaking take
you down" over and over.

863
00:42:46,228 --> 00:42:47,528
As the words came out,

864
00:42:47,529 --> 00:42:49,363
I kept locking eyes with Benjamin...

865
00:42:49,532 --> 00:42:51,599
and before I knew it,
I was pointing right at him.

866
00:42:51,600 --> 00:42:53,734
After that, I couldn't even keep
my rhymes together.

867
00:42:53,735 --> 00:42:56,036
Having him sitting right in front of me...

868
00:42:56,037 --> 00:42:57,805
completely threw me off.

869
00:42:58,673 --> 00:42:59,806
(Gray is talking about "Money.")

870
00:42:59,807 --> 00:43:01,208
Looking back on the past 44 years,

871
00:43:01,209 --> 00:43:04,645
I thought about what money
has meant to me.

872
00:43:05,213 --> 00:43:08,415
At one point, I was hooked on crypto.

873
00:43:08,684 --> 00:43:09,517
Oh.

874
00:43:09,518 --> 00:43:11,919
It wasn't for very long. About six months.

875
00:43:12,689 --> 00:43:15,623
I got into it
through a close friend's recommendation,

876
00:43:16,058 --> 00:43:17,959
and within six months,
I was totally hooked.

877
00:43:18,493 --> 00:43:20,461
When I was broke, I noticed something.

878
00:43:20,829 --> 00:43:23,364
Because I was desperate,

879
00:43:23,365 --> 00:43:26,267
I just kept making
the worst possible choices.

880
00:43:26,768 --> 00:43:28,569
Looking at the bigger picture,

881
00:43:28,970 --> 00:43:30,570
I wonder if having more people
in that state...

882
00:43:30,571 --> 00:43:33,573
is what fuels so much tension
in society today.

883
00:43:33,574 --> 00:43:35,108
That's what crossed my mind.

884
00:43:35,677 --> 00:43:38,245
(Life Speech: Money, Genie)

885
00:43:38,947 --> 00:43:40,314
Hello.

886
00:43:40,315 --> 00:43:44,585
I picked the keyword "Money,"

887
00:43:44,986 --> 00:43:46,887
but what I wrestled with was this.

888
00:43:47,788 --> 00:43:48,756
Since my code...

889
00:43:48,757 --> 00:43:51,258
was already pretty much out in the open...

890
00:43:51,259 --> 00:43:54,594
I wondered what I could say
that would actually resonate with you.

891
00:43:54,863 --> 00:43:56,897
I spent a lot of time thinking about it.

892
00:43:58,100 --> 00:44:01,936
It's true that I grew up...

893
00:44:01,937 --> 00:44:03,171
never wanting for anything,

894
00:44:03,338 --> 00:44:06,007
but I never viewed myself
as super wealthy.

895
00:44:06,008 --> 00:44:09,376
The reason is that so many of my friends
grew up like Banu.

896
00:44:09,377 --> 00:44:11,178
Some of them...

897
00:44:11,546 --> 00:44:14,515
own entire commercial buildings or...

898
00:44:14,516 --> 00:44:16,884
are decked out in designer labels
from head to toe.

899
00:44:16,885 --> 00:44:18,418
I have plenty of friends like that.

900
00:44:18,920 --> 00:44:20,888
Even the ones with jobs
still put everything on...

901
00:44:20,889 --> 00:44:23,023
their parents' credit cards.

902
00:44:23,024 --> 00:44:26,894
I'd tell them, "You're grown adults.
Why are you doing that?"

903
00:44:26,895 --> 00:44:29,897
And they'd reply, "We'll get hit
with a massive inheritance tax anyway,"

904
00:44:30,199 --> 00:44:32,700
"so we might as well spend it down now."

905
00:44:32,701 --> 00:44:35,269
"That way, when the estate passes down,
it won't be as much of a headache."

906
00:44:35,270 --> 00:44:36,103
Hearing that, I thought,

907
00:44:36,338 --> 00:44:38,463
"That kind of logic never even
crossed my mind,"

908
00:44:38,464 --> 00:44:39,951
"because I have nothing to inherit."

909
00:44:40,576 --> 00:44:42,676
Wow, people like that really exist.

910
00:44:42,978 --> 00:44:47,214
Anyway, my dad is very smart.

911
00:44:47,215 --> 00:44:51,919
He started an IT-related business
when he was young.

912
00:44:51,920 --> 00:44:53,754
But his business partner...

913
00:44:54,122 --> 00:44:56,290
sold my father's technology...

914
00:44:56,291 --> 00:44:59,359
and name to investors,

915
00:44:59,728 --> 00:45:02,730
and made off with all the money.

916
00:45:05,800 --> 00:45:08,568
So my dad started getting into trouble,

917
00:45:08,569 --> 00:45:10,537
and people started showing up
at our house...

918
00:45:10,537 --> 00:45:11,904
putting up seizure notices.

919
00:45:11,906 --> 00:45:14,274
I've experienced something like that too.

920
00:45:15,611 --> 00:45:18,579
So, at the time, I started reflecting.

921
00:45:19,080 --> 00:45:21,948
I realized
the things I'd taken for granted...

922
00:45:21,949 --> 00:45:23,916
weren't actually guaranteed.

923
00:45:23,917 --> 00:45:25,953
I realized that through little things...

924
00:45:25,954 --> 00:45:27,788
that happened back then.

925
00:45:27,789 --> 00:45:30,557
For example, Mom had a gift certificate...

926
00:45:30,558 --> 00:45:32,759
for a Chinese restaurant,

927
00:45:32,760 --> 00:45:34,161
worth 20 or 30 dollars...

928
00:45:34,162 --> 00:45:37,564
so we decided to eat out
for the first time in ages.

929
00:45:37,766 --> 00:45:39,233
But what could we get with that amount?

930
00:45:39,234 --> 00:45:40,734
Maybe a couple of bowls of noodles,

931
00:45:40,735 --> 00:45:43,704
but I really love Peking duck, actually.

932
00:45:44,107 --> 00:45:47,608
But obviously we couldn't afford it,

933
00:45:47,776 --> 00:45:49,744
so I couldn't order it.

934
00:45:50,512 --> 00:45:52,513
Then a couple sat down next to us...

935
00:45:52,514 --> 00:45:54,949
and casually ordered Peking duck.

936
00:45:55,250 --> 00:45:57,318
My inferiority complex exploded...

937
00:45:57,619 --> 00:46:01,055
and I cried that I wanted Peking duck.

938
00:46:01,056 --> 00:46:02,723
People still tease me about it.

939
00:46:02,724 --> 00:46:06,026
Saying that if I can't eat what I want,
I'll cry.

940
00:46:06,027 --> 00:46:07,328
They still tease me.

941
00:46:07,329 --> 00:46:10,664
I think it was the straw
that broke the camel's back.

942
00:46:11,299 --> 00:46:13,500
Why would you cry
because you couldn't eat Peking duck?

943
00:46:14,603 --> 00:46:17,771
"What can you eat at a Chinese restaurant
with 20 or 30 dollars?"

944
00:46:17,772 --> 00:46:19,540
You can eat enough noodles
to make you sick.

945
00:46:19,541 --> 00:46:22,143
You cried because you couldn't eat
Peking duck?

946
00:46:22,377 --> 00:46:25,012
How could that make you cry?

947
00:46:25,013 --> 00:46:26,780
A couple of bowls of noodles
is nothing to scoff at.

948
00:46:27,082 --> 00:46:28,549
Some people can't even afford that,

949
00:46:28,550 --> 00:46:31,218
so they save up
and get it on special occasions.

950
00:46:31,386 --> 00:46:33,987
I think our perspectives
are just different.

951
00:46:33,988 --> 00:46:38,160
(Right, equalism,
working class, conservative)

952
00:46:38,161 --> 00:46:40,261
I can't relate to her at all.

953
00:46:40,829 --> 00:46:43,230
But she mentioned the seizure notices...

954
00:46:43,231 --> 00:46:47,534
being stuck to her door and stuff...

955
00:46:47,535 --> 00:46:48,835
and I could relate.

956
00:46:49,037 --> 00:46:50,471
I work mostly in debt collection,

957
00:46:50,472 --> 00:46:52,940
and when we go to seize movable property,

958
00:46:53,375 --> 00:46:55,609
if they don't open the door,
we break it down.

959
00:46:55,810 --> 00:46:58,478
For someone experiencing that
for the first time,

960
00:46:58,880 --> 00:47:01,515
it can be extremely shocking.

961
00:47:01,716 --> 00:47:02,849
Despite going through all that,

962
00:47:02,850 --> 00:47:04,651
I think she should be proud
of the person she is today.

963
00:47:04,886 --> 00:47:07,321
And I realized she has also
had some struggles in life.

964
00:47:07,656 --> 00:47:09,857
What I realized
through that process was...

965
00:47:10,191 --> 00:47:12,827
People always say money,
given at weddings or funerals,

966
00:47:12,828 --> 00:47:16,730
reveals who your true friends are.

967
00:47:16,965 --> 00:47:19,700
If I thought
someone was a really close friend,

968
00:47:19,701 --> 00:47:21,769
but they gave me
almost nothing for my wedding,

969
00:47:22,338 --> 00:47:24,571
I'd naturally be disappointed.

970
00:47:25,073 --> 00:47:26,740
So I started thinking about how,

971
00:47:27,209 --> 00:47:29,810
even if we say love and other things...

972
00:47:29,811 --> 00:47:31,745
matter more than money,

973
00:47:32,080 --> 00:47:35,749
there are precious relationships
you can lose when you don't have money.

974
00:47:35,884 --> 00:47:38,552
I've thought about that a lot.

975
00:47:39,287 --> 00:47:44,592
"I really want to make a lot of money,"
is what I would say.

976
00:47:44,593 --> 00:47:48,428
So anyway, my dream for now
is to win the prize money.

977
00:47:48,797 --> 00:47:51,065
I hope we can all make a lot of money,

978
00:47:51,733 --> 00:47:52,866
and that I can too,

979
00:47:52,867 --> 00:47:55,936
and become a good person.

980
00:47:55,937 --> 00:47:57,237
Thank you.

981
00:48:01,976 --> 00:48:04,277
That was quite a relative perspective.

982
00:48:04,480 --> 00:48:07,281
From the perspective of someone
without money, it could sound like,

983
00:48:07,282 --> 00:48:10,417
"Wow, that's a privileged thing to say."

984
00:48:10,418 --> 00:48:12,620
But it's the life they experienced,

985
00:48:12,821 --> 00:48:15,089
and it didn't make me
uncomfortable at all.

986
00:48:15,090 --> 00:48:17,258
Either way,
there are ups and downs in life.

987
00:48:17,726 --> 00:48:19,493
When I talked about money,

988
00:48:19,494 --> 00:48:23,497
I was really careful thinking
about how people would take it.

989
00:48:23,798 --> 00:48:25,698
I thought about it a lot.

990
00:48:25,699 --> 00:48:27,434
But still,

991
00:48:27,435 --> 00:48:30,504
I didn't want to make things up.

992
00:48:30,672 --> 00:48:33,375
I just spoke candidly
about how money affected my life.

993
00:48:33,376 --> 00:48:35,709
That's really all I was trying to do.

994
00:48:35,710 --> 00:48:37,645
But...

995
00:48:37,646 --> 00:48:40,214
I don't know how it came across.

996
00:48:40,649 --> 00:48:42,883
The third round of Life Speeches
will begin shortly.

997
00:48:43,118 --> 00:48:45,853
The third round will now begin.

998
00:48:45,854 --> 00:48:47,888
(Life Speech: Inferiority, Sugar)

999
00:48:49,491 --> 00:48:51,125
Hello, I'm Sugar.

1000
00:48:52,627 --> 00:48:53,994
- I chose...
- She got louder!

1001
00:48:54,229 --> 00:48:57,331
"Inferiority" as my speech topic.

1002
00:48:57,532 --> 00:48:59,600
When people hear "inferiority,"

1003
00:48:59,601 --> 00:49:02,169
they usually think of it
as a very negative emotion.

1004
00:49:02,504 --> 00:49:05,172
But my inferiority complex...

1005
00:49:05,173 --> 00:49:07,608
has actually been the driving force...

1006
00:49:07,776 --> 00:49:09,643
helping me get through life.

1007
00:49:10,478 --> 00:49:13,680
I was born second out of three sisters.

1008
00:49:14,015 --> 00:49:18,018
I wasn't as pretty as my older sister...

1009
00:49:18,019 --> 00:49:20,087
nor as smart as my younger one.

1010
00:49:20,288 --> 00:49:24,124
So naturally,
I grew up being compared to them.

1011
00:49:25,226 --> 00:49:28,528
But even so,
if I had one natural advantage,

1012
00:49:28,529 --> 00:49:30,897
it was my positive attitude.

1013
00:49:32,167 --> 00:49:35,536
When I'm with people,
I don't try to put my needs first.

1014
00:49:35,537 --> 00:49:38,839
I'm good at helping people instead.

1015
00:49:40,241 --> 00:49:42,809
I'm also a "Maxim" model,

1016
00:49:43,077 --> 00:49:46,714
which was something I challenged myself...

1017
00:49:46,715 --> 00:49:48,548
to overcome my inferiority complex.

1018
00:49:49,343 --> 00:49:51,551
I didn't think anyone looked at me
thinking,

1019
00:49:51,552 --> 00:49:55,489
"Wow, she's sexy,"
or "She should be a 'Maxim' model."

1020
00:49:56,558 --> 00:49:57,491
Did anyone?

1021
00:49:57,792 --> 00:49:58,658
"Did anyone?"

1022
00:50:00,528 --> 00:50:03,630
At first,

1023
00:50:03,631 --> 00:50:05,565
even the photographers...

1024
00:50:05,566 --> 00:50:07,167
found me difficult to shoot.

1025
00:50:07,602 --> 00:50:11,495
They said I was a model
who understood things in her head,

1026
00:50:11,496 --> 00:50:12,806
but couldn't express them well.

1027
00:50:13,975 --> 00:50:16,577
And I felt really intimidated...

1028
00:50:16,578 --> 00:50:18,779
by models with incredible physiques.

1029
00:50:19,147 --> 00:50:20,980
I got so intimidated that one time...

1030
00:50:20,981 --> 00:50:24,051
I brought along my favorite alcohol.

1031
00:50:24,619 --> 00:50:27,321
I even drank some before the shoot...

1032
00:50:27,322 --> 00:50:29,890
in the changing room.

1033
00:50:29,891 --> 00:50:30,958
She really tried.

1034
00:50:30,959 --> 00:50:33,493
I even ended up winning a contest, too.

1035
00:50:34,128 --> 00:50:37,730
Anyway,
my story isn't anything extraordinary,

1036
00:50:37,731 --> 00:50:41,134
but I hope my positive energy
comes across to all of you.

1037
00:50:41,569 --> 00:50:43,770
If you're having a hard time, call me.

1038
00:50:43,939 --> 00:50:47,408
I'll always have a drink with you...

1039
00:50:48,042 --> 00:50:49,676
and be your friend.

1040
00:50:49,677 --> 00:50:53,113
Time is going by way slower
than I expected.

1041
00:50:54,983 --> 00:50:56,227
What should I do?

1042
00:50:57,385 --> 00:51:00,620
I thought I'd fill the full 15 minutes...

1043
00:51:02,191 --> 00:51:04,758
- This is a big problem...
- Only four minutes passed.

1044
00:51:06,227 --> 00:51:08,094
It's only been four minutes, right?

1045
00:51:10,465 --> 00:51:13,500
So I hope we can stay close
even after we leave here.

1046
00:51:13,501 --> 00:51:14,601
This was...

1047
00:51:15,303 --> 00:51:18,138
your lump of inferiority, Sugar.

1048
00:51:19,541 --> 00:51:20,941
"Lump of inferiority!"

1049
00:51:25,313 --> 00:51:28,015
There were some things
from Sugar's speech...

1050
00:51:28,016 --> 00:51:30,851
that I noticed I had in common with her.

1051
00:51:30,852 --> 00:51:31,852
I'm the second child too,

1052
00:51:31,853 --> 00:51:34,855
so I thought,
"I know exactly what she means."

1053
00:51:35,924 --> 00:51:39,293
She wrote the whole script, read it over,

1054
00:51:39,294 --> 00:51:43,230
and thought,
"Yes, this is a 15-minute script!"

1055
00:51:44,098 --> 00:51:48,201
Imagining her preparing it
like that is really funny.

1056
00:51:48,703 --> 00:51:50,137
I really enjoyed it.

1057
00:51:50,405 --> 00:51:52,272
I heard afterward that...

1058
00:51:52,273 --> 00:51:56,276
she did calculate the time
while writing it.

1059
00:51:59,247 --> 00:52:00,280
Sugar...

1060
00:52:00,815 --> 00:52:01,816
was really cute.

1061
00:52:01,817 --> 00:52:03,316
The best part was
when she talked about "Maxim."

1062
00:52:03,484 --> 00:52:04,584
I thought...

1063
00:52:04,852 --> 00:52:06,319
I should check out her photos.

1064
00:52:07,342 --> 00:52:09,175
I want to see what got her the win.

1065
00:52:09,580 --> 00:52:10,222
Yes.

1066
00:52:10,491 --> 00:52:11,425
Yes, she's attractive.

1067
00:52:12,226 --> 00:52:16,129
But she's not my type,

1068
00:52:16,130 --> 00:52:18,097
so it's not that kind of attraction.

1069
00:52:18,098 --> 00:52:20,200
I meant I think of her as a friend.

1070
00:52:24,872 --> 00:52:29,476
(Life Speech: Inferiority, Dark Knight)

1071
00:52:30,077 --> 00:52:33,013
Please enter the chat room.

1072
00:52:33,915 --> 00:52:35,983
What, why are you taking the chair?

1073
00:52:41,494 --> 00:52:43,295
What's with that chair?

1074
00:52:43,381 --> 00:52:46,193
- What will he show?
- Is this a talent show or something?

1075
00:52:51,599 --> 00:52:52,999
What did he prepare?

1076
00:53:02,010 --> 00:53:04,478
To help with nerves,

1077
00:53:05,013 --> 00:53:07,214
I'm wearing sunglasses for my speech.

1078
00:53:09,017 --> 00:53:11,285
I've been here with all of you.

1079
00:53:12,186 --> 00:53:14,754
And I've survived this far...

1080
00:53:14,922 --> 00:53:17,257
by stubbornly insisting
on my ridiculous convictions.

1081
00:53:17,692 --> 00:53:19,793
I think everyone here...

1082
00:53:19,994 --> 00:53:23,063
has a little more natural talent than me.

1083
00:53:23,898 --> 00:53:25,131
For me, my talent was...

1084
00:53:25,700 --> 00:53:28,902
being able to watch something once
and imitate it easily.

1085
00:53:29,671 --> 00:53:33,273
For example,
if I wanted to take up a sport,

1086
00:53:33,675 --> 00:53:36,577
I could pick it up in only a month.

1087
00:53:37,377 --> 00:53:40,413
When I was young,
people even called me a genius.

1088
00:53:40,715 --> 00:53:42,183
And what happened then is that...

1089
00:53:42,184 --> 00:53:44,051
I got scouted a lot.

1090
00:53:44,386 --> 00:53:46,520
I had countless opportunities.

1091
00:53:47,022 --> 00:53:49,289
The baseball coach would say,
"Play baseball."

1092
00:53:49,358 --> 00:53:51,492
The track coach would say, "Do track."

1093
00:53:52,292 --> 00:53:54,460
"Go home and tell your parents."

1094
00:53:55,396 --> 00:53:57,063
I couldn't say anything.

1095
00:53:57,800 --> 00:53:58,799
Why?

1096
00:53:58,800 --> 00:54:01,101
Because I already knew I couldn't do it.

1097
00:54:03,438 --> 00:54:05,873
I think I grew up very quickly.

1098
00:54:06,374 --> 00:54:10,010
I knew my family's situation,
so I couldn't say anything.

1099
00:54:11,946 --> 00:54:14,548
We had mice running around our house
when I was young.

1100
00:54:15,950 --> 00:54:17,728
So we always set traps...

1101
00:54:18,352 --> 00:54:20,219
and we didn't have a boiler.

1102
00:54:20,588 --> 00:54:23,089
We had to get up at dawn
and check the briquettes.

1103
00:54:23,090 --> 00:54:25,358
Why? We were afraid
of carbon monoxide poisoning.

1104
00:54:28,229 --> 00:54:31,264
I lived a life full of inferiority.

1105
00:54:31,833 --> 00:54:34,167
When I started elementary school,
for the first time,

1106
00:54:34,669 --> 00:54:36,370
I heard of "Santa Claus," I think.

1107
00:54:37,772 --> 00:54:39,606
Because my parents had never told me...

1108
00:54:39,675 --> 00:54:44,278
stories about Santa
or magical Christmas stories.

1109
00:54:46,380 --> 00:54:49,048
I've never really enjoyed life.

1110
00:54:49,551 --> 00:54:52,118
I've never done the things I wanted to do.

1111
00:54:52,620 --> 00:54:54,054
Because I grew up like that...

1112
00:54:54,489 --> 00:54:56,056
none of your meetings...

1113
00:54:56,057 --> 00:54:58,292
were important to me. Why?

1114
00:54:59,827 --> 00:55:02,529
Someone like me has to move up quickly.

1115
00:55:03,331 --> 00:55:04,899
I had to move up quickly,

1116
00:55:04,900 --> 00:55:07,401
to reach the level
where I could eat and work alongside you.

1117
00:55:07,602 --> 00:55:09,102
Even getting there...

1118
00:55:09,470 --> 00:55:12,005
meant taking a very different path,

1119
00:55:12,407 --> 00:55:14,707
while you were sitting there from birth.

1120
00:55:17,278 --> 00:55:18,712
I'm so jealous of you.

1121
00:55:19,379 --> 00:55:24,350
These aren't the conditions
that lower-class people face.

1122
00:55:25,553 --> 00:55:28,355
You're all playing in upper-class society.

1123
00:55:28,562 --> 00:55:29,660
How nice is that?

1124
00:55:29,661 --> 00:55:31,362
Barely lift a finger,
and the money piles up.

1125
00:55:31,363 --> 00:55:33,628
Just a few strokes of a pen,
and goods are brought right to us.

1126
00:55:36,130 --> 00:55:39,466
There was one reason
I joined the military.

1127
00:55:39,834 --> 00:55:43,537
It was a chance for me
to move up in society.

1128
00:55:45,673 --> 00:55:47,440
That's why I joined the Special Forces.

1129
00:55:47,776 --> 00:55:50,544
Everyone had the same conditions,

1130
00:55:50,545 --> 00:55:52,511
lived in the same controlled environment.

1131
00:55:52,512 --> 00:55:54,981
And to me, the military was
a land of dreams and hope.

1132
00:55:55,449 --> 00:55:57,617
Why?
Because everyone was under the same rules.

1133
00:55:57,985 --> 00:56:00,286
So I thought
if I just put in a little more time,

1134
00:56:00,955 --> 00:56:01,955
I'd be able to make it.

1135
00:56:01,956 --> 00:56:04,758
Working out at dawn and at night.

1136
00:56:04,959 --> 00:56:07,127
You've all heard
of the 400-kilometer forced march, right?

1137
00:56:07,361 --> 00:56:09,495
It's an eight-night,
nine-day training exercise...

1138
00:56:09,765 --> 00:56:12,132
where you walk 400 kilometers.

1139
00:56:12,492 --> 00:56:14,611
As soon as I got back from it,

1140
00:56:14,612 --> 00:56:17,370
I dropped my bag
and ran straight to the gym.

1141
00:56:18,406 --> 00:56:19,973
I lived military life so intensely...

1142
00:56:20,308 --> 00:56:22,676
that among all the things
they wanted us to do,

1143
00:56:22,677 --> 00:56:25,279
there was hardly a single one
I didn't rank first in.

1144
00:56:25,414 --> 00:56:27,314
I pushed myself that hard.

1145
00:56:27,315 --> 00:56:28,415
(Rope climbing, push-ups, chin-ups)

1146
00:56:28,783 --> 00:56:31,385
I lived intensely, prepared intensely,

1147
00:56:31,386 --> 00:56:34,721
and beat people one by one,

1148
00:56:35,256 --> 00:56:36,316
until suddenly,

1149
00:56:37,236 --> 00:56:40,960
everyone started listening to me.

1150
00:56:43,564 --> 00:56:45,465
"Money doesn't make you happy," you say?

1151
00:56:46,334 --> 00:56:47,501
Have you ever been poor, then?

1152
00:56:47,635 --> 00:56:49,636
Money can't represent happiness, I know.

1153
00:56:50,371 --> 00:56:52,405
But if you have no money,
you're definitely unhappy.

1154
00:56:54,141 --> 00:56:56,843
Any talk about happiness
without having lived an unhappy life,

1155
00:56:57,211 --> 00:57:00,813
in my opinion, is invalid.

1156
00:57:00,948 --> 00:57:04,784
And to all the young people watching this,

1157
00:57:04,919 --> 00:57:07,153
and those
born into circumstances like mine,

1158
00:57:07,688 --> 00:57:09,722
keep going. I'll show you.

1159
00:57:10,024 --> 00:57:12,826
Even if you're born slow or disadvantaged,

1160
00:57:13,261 --> 00:57:14,861
I'll show you how far you can go.

1161
00:57:16,631 --> 00:57:19,165
This has been Dark Knight.

1162
00:57:21,235 --> 00:57:22,235
Thank you.

1163
00:57:23,204 --> 00:57:25,205
(Inferiority)

1164
00:57:26,241 --> 00:57:29,142
I didn't have a childhood...

1165
00:57:29,143 --> 00:57:31,845
as difficult as Dark Knight's,

1166
00:57:31,846 --> 00:57:34,715
but still, since I was a kid...

1167
00:57:34,716 --> 00:57:36,650
I also couldn't ask for what I wanted.

1168
00:57:36,651 --> 00:57:39,953
Due to my family's finances,
I had to give things up.

1169
00:57:40,253 --> 00:57:43,589
And at the same time,
I had to work really hard.

1170
00:57:43,590 --> 00:57:46,692
I didn't have the luxury
to think things over,

1171
00:57:47,094 --> 00:57:49,563
or consider other options.

1172
00:57:49,564 --> 00:57:52,666
His past really overlapped with mine.

1173
00:57:52,801 --> 00:57:57,537
When he described how hard he had to work,
I really understood what he meant.

1174
00:57:57,638 --> 00:58:00,373
I felt like he saw things
very similarly to me.

1175
00:58:00,374 --> 00:58:02,041
I really did.

1176
00:58:02,276 --> 00:58:03,776
Of course,

1177
00:58:03,777 --> 00:58:05,511
I believe everyone
has hardships of their own.

1178
00:58:05,713 --> 00:58:08,981
Saying, "I've suffered this much,"

1179
00:58:09,216 --> 00:58:11,483
"so your problem..."

1180
00:58:11,484 --> 00:58:13,986
"is nothing compared to mine."

1181
00:58:13,987 --> 00:58:16,889
I don't think that's respectful.

1182
00:58:16,890 --> 00:58:19,792
But after hearing Dark Knight today,

1183
00:58:19,793 --> 00:58:22,129
I felt I understood a little...

1184
00:58:22,130 --> 00:58:25,164
of how much he had to struggle.

1185
00:58:25,565 --> 00:58:27,133
So I could understand...

1186
00:58:27,435 --> 00:58:29,302
some of that discomfort and move past it.

1187
00:58:30,471 --> 00:58:34,107
I really connected with the story,
and I almost cried.

1188
00:58:34,108 --> 00:58:39,012
He lived almost
the exact opposite life from mine,

1189
00:58:39,414 --> 00:58:41,948
so I felt weirdly sorry.

1190
00:58:41,949 --> 00:58:44,282
It was like we were literally
on opposite sides of the world...

1191
00:58:44,283 --> 00:58:45,930
when we were young.

1192
00:58:45,931 --> 00:58:47,787
Still, while I was giving my speech,

1193
00:58:48,088 --> 00:58:51,624
I talked about things
like happiness and money.

1194
00:58:52,026 --> 00:58:55,395
From his perspective,
it might have seemed really trivial.

1195
00:58:55,396 --> 00:58:57,664
So I felt sorry about that.

1196
00:58:58,332 --> 00:59:00,767
In Dark Knight's case,

1197
00:59:00,935 --> 00:59:04,805
he seems to have some resentment...

1198
00:59:04,806 --> 00:59:08,274
toward people who grew up wealthy.

1199
00:59:08,275 --> 00:59:13,313
I tried to be as honest as I could...

1200
00:59:13,314 --> 00:59:15,782
but I wondered if I offended anyone.

1201
00:59:15,783 --> 00:59:18,718
Could it still make someone feel bad?

1202
00:59:19,254 --> 00:59:21,238
So it's embarrassing.

1203
00:59:21,938 --> 00:59:23,048
Seriously.

1204
00:59:24,158 --> 00:59:27,160
Superman.

1205
00:59:27,629 --> 00:59:30,497
Please enter the chat room.

1206
00:59:31,198 --> 00:59:32,732
I'm nervous. Really nervous.

1207
00:59:32,900 --> 00:59:34,868
(One hour earlier)

1208
00:59:38,211 --> 00:59:39,545
This is really nerve-racking.

1209
00:59:39,784 --> 00:59:41,808
I've never seen you nervous before.

1210
00:59:41,809 --> 00:59:44,043
Me neither. What's with you?

1211
00:59:47,748 --> 00:59:50,049
I think this is the first time
I've ever been this nervous.

1212
00:59:50,317 --> 00:59:52,485
I have to talk
about things I've never talked about,

1213
00:59:52,621 --> 00:59:54,587
and reveal my darkest aspects.

1214
00:59:55,022 --> 00:59:57,123
That's what I had to do,

1215
00:59:57,491 --> 00:59:59,959
so I guess I was scared.

1216
01:00:01,829 --> 01:00:05,031
(Life Speech: Inferiority, Superman)

1217
01:00:06,434 --> 01:00:09,035
Please begin your speech.

1218
01:00:11,505 --> 01:00:12,449
Can you hear me?

1219
01:00:15,142 --> 01:00:16,042
Yes.

1220
01:00:17,812 --> 01:00:19,446
I'm really nervous today.

1221
01:00:21,515 --> 01:00:22,748
I've never seen him like this.

1222
01:00:26,854 --> 01:00:29,923
I have a huge inferiority complex.

1223
01:00:29,924 --> 01:00:32,792
A lot of people probably think I don't,

1224
01:00:33,111 --> 01:00:34,111
but I actually do.

1225
01:00:34,112 --> 01:00:35,663
I lost 11 to 2,

1226
01:00:35,664 --> 01:00:39,199
so you all gave me
a huge inferiority complex.

1227
01:00:40,768 --> 01:00:43,269
Yes. Even here,
I'm living with an inferiority complex.

1228
01:00:44,505 --> 01:00:47,073
I titled my speech,

1229
01:00:47,341 --> 01:00:48,441
"A Survival Story..."

1230
01:00:48,442 --> 01:00:51,711
"of a Seoul National University
Law Graduate Who Never Became a Lawyer."

1231
01:00:52,731 --> 01:00:53,411
Right.

1232
01:00:53,844 --> 01:00:54,647
I...

1233
01:00:55,016 --> 01:00:56,582
I'm the first person in my family...

1234
01:00:56,784 --> 01:00:59,119
to attend a four-year university.

1235
01:01:00,121 --> 01:01:01,822
My parents...

1236
01:01:01,989 --> 01:01:04,590
didn't attend prestigious schools,

1237
01:01:04,925 --> 01:01:07,526
but they worked extremely hard.

1238
01:01:08,429 --> 01:01:10,363
They made sure I could study...

1239
01:01:10,798 --> 01:01:12,999
without lacking anything.

1240
01:01:14,335 --> 01:01:15,468
I have five brothers.

1241
01:01:15,870 --> 01:01:16,903
Five?

1242
01:01:17,405 --> 01:01:20,673
I was the eldest child,

1243
01:01:21,075 --> 01:01:23,777
so I had everyone's eyes on me.

1244
01:01:23,911 --> 01:01:27,180
I worked hard and got
into a four-year university,

1245
01:01:27,882 --> 01:01:30,159
but studying subjects
I had zero interest in...

1246
01:01:30,184 --> 01:01:31,685
was pure misery.

1247
01:01:32,219 --> 01:01:34,020
Still, with so many family members...

1248
01:01:34,388 --> 01:01:37,056
pinning their hopes on me,

1249
01:01:37,425 --> 01:01:39,593
I kept pushing through
and studying like crazy.

1250
01:01:39,693 --> 01:01:41,361
I know the topic today is "Inferiority,"

1251
01:01:41,362 --> 01:01:43,562
but I did manage to graduate with honors.

1252
01:01:44,165 --> 01:01:48,301
Even so, I desperately needed...
an escape route back then,

1253
01:01:48,569 --> 01:01:49,936
so right after graduation,

1254
01:01:50,571 --> 01:01:52,972
I went straight to enlist, but...

1255
01:01:54,985 --> 01:01:57,185
it turned out I had severe mobility issues
in both shoulders.

1256
01:01:57,210 --> 01:01:58,677
(Due to injury from a judo match)

1257
01:01:58,678 --> 01:02:02,181
So I was placed in alternative service,
doing public service work.

1258
01:02:02,182 --> 01:02:05,184
Even while serving,
I was under such intense stress...

1259
01:02:05,486 --> 01:02:08,488
that I kept on eating. That's how I lived.

1260
01:02:08,489 --> 01:02:09,387
Then,

1261
01:02:10,357 --> 01:02:12,292
by the time my two years of service ended,

1262
01:02:12,293 --> 01:02:14,760
I was suddenly staring down thirty.

1263
01:02:14,761 --> 01:02:18,798
That was when my inferiority complex
really began to fester.

1264
01:02:19,133 --> 01:02:22,202
By that point, some of my peers...

1265
01:02:22,804 --> 01:02:26,072
had already become judges, prosecutors,

1266
01:02:26,073 --> 01:02:27,607
or corporate lawyers,

1267
01:02:28,108 --> 01:02:31,510
while I felt like an absolute failure,

1268
01:02:31,511 --> 01:02:34,580
completely left behind.

1269
01:02:34,581 --> 01:02:36,809
People around me wondered
why I hadn't made anything of myself...

1270
01:02:36,834 --> 01:02:38,261
as an SNU law graduate.

1271
01:02:38,285 --> 01:02:40,987
Having to endure those kinds of looks...

1272
01:02:40,988 --> 01:02:43,990
went on for a very long time.

1273
01:02:43,991 --> 01:02:45,558
It was an agonizing period,

1274
01:02:46,059 --> 01:02:49,095
but it was still the path I had chosen.

1275
01:02:49,396 --> 01:02:50,631
So I just threw myself...

1276
01:02:50,965 --> 01:02:52,566
into whatever odd jobs I could get.

1277
01:02:52,567 --> 01:02:53,934
I ran a street stall,

1278
01:02:54,535 --> 01:02:56,035
worked low-level corporate gigs,

1279
01:02:56,971 --> 01:03:01,074
and some of you might know
what this feels like,

1280
01:03:01,075 --> 01:03:04,310
but I couldn't bring myself to attend
friends' weddings or family gatherings.

1281
01:03:04,311 --> 01:03:06,580
I just couldn't afford the gift money.

1282
01:03:06,581 --> 01:03:10,083
Missing out on
so many close friends' weddings...

1283
01:03:10,084 --> 01:03:12,619
really took a heavy emotional toll.

1284
01:03:12,620 --> 01:03:17,190
Watching all my peers thrive
and move forward...

1285
01:03:17,725 --> 01:03:19,493
while I was just scraping by...

1286
01:03:19,994 --> 01:03:22,129
feeling like an unemployed nobody
after college.

1287
01:03:26,066 --> 01:03:29,135
Under the weight of everyone
looking down on me,

1288
01:03:30,939 --> 01:03:32,806
the only outlet I had...

1289
01:03:32,807 --> 01:03:36,176
was devouring books.

1290
01:03:36,543 --> 01:03:38,777
I consumed them constantly.

1291
01:03:39,547 --> 01:03:42,515
I read anything and everything
I could lay my hands on,

1292
01:03:42,516 --> 01:03:44,593
trying desperately to drown out
the gnawing inadequacy inside.

1293
01:03:44,618 --> 01:03:46,719
I told myself I had to gain real insight,

1294
01:03:46,720 --> 01:03:48,988
which at that stage...

1295
01:04:00,301 --> 01:04:02,235
Either way, I endured.

1296
01:04:02,903 --> 01:04:03,803
I held on,

1297
01:04:04,639 --> 01:04:07,407
and after reading so extensively...

1298
01:04:07,708 --> 01:04:09,209
a new realization began to take shape.

1299
01:04:09,376 --> 01:04:11,445
There are countless ways
to contribute to society...

1300
01:04:11,446 --> 01:04:14,148
while pursuing work
that genuinely matters to you.

1301
01:04:14,415 --> 01:04:19,386
But because of societal expectations
and rigid career paths,

1302
01:04:19,621 --> 01:04:21,722
so many people are forced
to give up what they truly want.

1303
01:04:21,723 --> 01:04:23,122
That thought consumed me.

1304
01:04:25,092 --> 01:04:27,494
A society structured like that...

1305
01:04:28,329 --> 01:04:29,229
I'm sorry.

1306
01:04:34,768 --> 01:04:38,003
Anyway, I became obsessed
with building that kind of society.

1307
01:04:38,772 --> 01:04:42,308
That eventually led to an incredible
opportunity to enter politics.

1308
01:04:42,976 --> 01:04:43,911
But once I was in,

1309
01:04:44,140 --> 01:04:45,921
that same inferiority complex
reared its head again.

1310
01:04:45,946 --> 01:04:48,953
Entering the world
of active politics meant...

1311
01:04:48,978 --> 01:04:51,727
facing a whole new layer
of scrutiny and competition.

1312
01:04:51,752 --> 01:04:54,086
Polar Bear might understand this feeling.

1313
01:04:54,988 --> 01:04:57,790
Somewhere along the line,
I became convinced...

1314
01:04:57,791 --> 01:04:59,559
that I had to be flawless.

1315
01:04:59,560 --> 01:05:03,996
If you've ever felt
that I'm overly rigid or stubborn,

1316
01:05:04,598 --> 01:05:07,868
it's because my entire twenties
taught me that if that shield broke,

1317
01:05:08,202 --> 01:05:10,304
my self-esteem would crumble
right along with it.

1318
01:05:11,004 --> 01:05:12,671
Looking back,

1319
01:05:12,873 --> 01:05:15,174
that stubbornness is probably
the very reason...

1320
01:05:15,175 --> 01:05:17,343
I didn't get elected.

1321
01:05:17,846 --> 01:05:21,347
So I wanted to take this moment
to give you that context...

1322
01:05:21,348 --> 01:05:22,982
and sincerely apologize.

1323
01:05:24,618 --> 01:05:26,319
As for Superman,

1324
01:05:26,320 --> 01:05:30,924
we've crossed paths for a long time
in political circles.

1325
01:05:31,125 --> 01:05:33,493
We've shared the same sphere
and been quite close.

1326
01:05:33,494 --> 01:05:35,061
But Superman is notoriously guarded...

1327
01:05:35,062 --> 01:05:36,763
when it comes to his personal struggles...

1328
01:05:37,099 --> 01:05:38,765
or vulnerabilities.

1329
01:05:38,919 --> 01:05:40,820
He rarely shows that side of himself.

1330
01:05:41,134 --> 01:05:42,869
So hearing him open up
about that whole journey...

1331
01:05:43,103 --> 01:05:45,871
and lay bare how painful it really was,

1332
01:05:45,872 --> 01:05:48,308
showed me a side of him
I'd never seen before.

1333
01:05:48,708 --> 01:05:50,443
In my twenties, I also took
an unconventional road...

1334
01:05:50,444 --> 01:05:52,878
that most people steered clear of,

1335
01:05:52,879 --> 01:05:55,533
and I remember
the raw instability and fear...

1336
01:05:55,558 --> 01:05:57,583
that came with that gamble.

1337
01:05:57,584 --> 01:05:59,852
Because of that,
his story hit very close to home.

1338
01:05:59,853 --> 01:06:01,121
I saw my own younger self in him.

1339
01:06:01,156 --> 01:06:03,289
It felt like my entire twenties...

1340
01:06:03,290 --> 01:06:05,158
were flashing right before my eyes.

1341
01:06:05,926 --> 01:06:09,896
Superman is actually an upperclassman
from my university.

1342
01:06:10,030 --> 01:06:11,230
(Benjamin, lawyer)

1343
01:06:11,231 --> 01:06:12,531
Exactly...

1344
01:06:13,101 --> 01:06:14,201
He carries that distinct aura...

1345
01:06:14,435 --> 01:06:16,703
of an SNU law alum who isn't an attorney.

1346
01:06:16,830 --> 01:06:19,632
I have other close upperclassmen like him.

1347
01:06:19,783 --> 01:06:22,251
That established dynamic makes things
a bit tricky from the start,

1348
01:06:22,276 --> 01:06:24,244
if you're surrounded by high achievers.

1349
01:06:24,245 --> 01:06:25,812
You don't need to think that way.

1350
01:06:25,846 --> 01:06:28,692
Law wasn't my original major,
but it clicked with me,

1351
01:06:28,716 --> 01:06:31,417
so I went to law school
and became an attorney.

1352
01:06:31,584 --> 01:06:33,819
Entering politics is honestly
on a completely different level.

1353
01:06:33,820 --> 01:06:35,261
It's far more brutal than legal practice,

1354
01:06:35,286 --> 01:06:37,122
where you simply formulate
the right arguments.

1355
01:06:37,123 --> 01:06:39,358
The landscape is so much wider.

1356
01:06:39,560 --> 01:06:41,661
I found it deeply admirable...

1357
01:06:42,664 --> 01:06:44,540
to see him dedicate himself
so fiercely to it.

1358
01:06:44,565 --> 01:06:46,799
But Superman is only human after all.

1359
01:06:47,469 --> 01:06:49,969
The peer pressure around him
must have been crushing.

1360
01:06:50,137 --> 01:06:51,238
That couldn't have been easy.

1361
01:06:51,705 --> 01:06:55,309
Hearing those raw personal confessions...

1362
01:06:55,310 --> 01:06:57,510
really resonated with me.

1363
01:06:57,878 --> 01:06:59,880
Well, all I can do now is
focus on my own game.

1364
01:07:02,449 --> 01:07:05,651
Benjamin.

1365
01:07:05,652 --> 01:07:08,654
Please step into the chat room.

1366
01:07:17,731 --> 01:07:18,965
I was genuinely intrigued.

1367
01:07:18,966 --> 01:07:21,167
What would his opening line be?

1368
01:07:21,301 --> 01:07:23,535
I wanted to see what kind
of play he was setting up.

1369
01:07:23,570 --> 01:07:26,639
I couldn't wait to see his angle.

1370
01:07:36,984 --> 01:07:38,318
Oh, he put on glasses.

1371
01:07:39,620 --> 01:07:41,054
Out of nowhere, he threw on glasses.

1372
01:07:45,959 --> 01:07:47,427
I'm Benjamin.

1373
01:07:50,864 --> 01:07:53,165
I think it's time to address
the elephant in the room.

1374
01:07:53,333 --> 01:07:54,266
Just like that?

1375
01:07:55,302 --> 01:07:56,269
Inferiority...

1376
01:07:57,371 --> 01:07:59,672
or having a "sense of inadequacy."

1377
01:08:00,208 --> 01:08:02,174
I don't think I need
to lean on English anymore.

1378
01:08:06,280 --> 01:08:07,480
The Ben routine is officially over.

1379
01:08:07,481 --> 01:08:08,949
I'm perfectly fluent in Korean.

1380
01:08:14,688 --> 01:08:15,488
He dropped the act.

1381
01:08:15,489 --> 01:08:18,858
First, the harsh reality is...

1382
01:08:20,060 --> 01:08:21,828
every single one of you suspects me...

1383
01:08:22,129 --> 01:08:24,163
and my back is against the wall.

1384
01:08:25,566 --> 01:08:27,066
So this is the hand he's playing?

1385
01:08:27,301 --> 01:08:29,769
Since I'm already
your prime suspect for the spy,

1386
01:08:29,770 --> 01:08:31,871
let's just assume I am one.

1387
01:08:33,173 --> 01:08:36,075
He's tackling it head-on.

1388
01:08:37,311 --> 01:08:40,280
When Nangja was eliminated yesterday,

1389
01:08:40,281 --> 01:08:42,081
I was genuinely heartbroken.

1390
01:08:42,783 --> 01:08:46,051
But was she not blatantly
lying to everyone?

1391
01:08:46,920 --> 01:08:49,188
It's an uncomfortable fact
everyone already knows,

1392
01:08:49,189 --> 01:08:51,224
even if nobody wanted
to call her a liar to her face.

1393
01:08:51,458 --> 01:08:52,658
He's smooth with this.

1394
01:08:52,659 --> 01:08:54,127
He makes a compelling case.

1395
01:08:54,428 --> 01:08:56,796
Why did Nangja...

1396
01:08:57,898 --> 01:08:59,932
act so suspiciously right until the end?

1397
01:09:01,201 --> 01:09:02,234
Why did Nangja...

1398
01:09:02,936 --> 01:09:04,237
claim she had figured out...

1399
01:09:04,262 --> 01:09:06,906
who the spy was,
yet refused to say the name?

1400
01:09:08,175 --> 01:09:09,818
Have any of you stopped
to think about that?

1401
01:09:09,843 --> 01:09:11,386
Was it because she didn't feel like it?

1402
01:09:11,411 --> 01:09:13,746
Or was there a compelling reason
she couldn't?

1403
01:09:13,747 --> 01:09:14,813
Did she actually know?

1404
01:09:14,814 --> 01:09:17,316
Or was she deliberately withholding it?

1405
01:09:18,452 --> 01:09:20,028
All three could hold weight,

1406
01:09:20,053 --> 01:09:23,455
but Nangja had more than enough motive...

1407
01:09:23,957 --> 01:09:27,259
to keep the spy's identity
completely buried.

1408
01:09:28,095 --> 01:09:29,762
Exposing it offered her zero advantage,

1409
01:09:30,130 --> 01:09:32,998
while keeping it quiet
posed no threat to anyone else.

1410
01:09:33,600 --> 01:09:35,301
If catching a spy is that straightforward,

1411
01:09:35,302 --> 01:09:36,836
you just call them out and wrap it up.

1412
01:09:36,837 --> 01:09:38,737
Drop the hammer on the spy and game over.

1413
01:09:38,972 --> 01:09:39,838
Except...

1414
01:09:40,173 --> 01:09:41,608
that isn't how this game is built.

1415
01:09:42,109 --> 01:09:44,510
You are all operating
under a massive misconception.

1416
01:09:45,078 --> 01:09:47,981
This game doesn't just end
because you root out a spy.

1417
01:09:48,815 --> 01:09:51,884
Nor does it end when the spy knocks out...

1418
01:09:52,319 --> 01:09:53,553
a bunch of residents.

1419
01:09:54,721 --> 01:10:00,292
Whether the original spy survives
all the way to the finish line...

1420
01:10:01,428 --> 01:10:02,495
changes virtually nothing.

1421
01:10:02,496 --> 01:10:04,765
Because the role itself
cannot be permanently eliminated.

1422
01:10:06,033 --> 01:10:07,200
If I were indeed the spy,

1423
01:10:07,201 --> 01:10:09,535
here is a clue I can afford to give you.

1424
01:10:09,536 --> 01:10:10,670
A massive piece of intel.

1425
01:10:10,771 --> 01:10:12,138
If a spy gets eliminated,

1426
01:10:12,139 --> 01:10:13,874
a replacement immediately
steps into their shoes.

1427
01:10:17,110 --> 01:10:20,179
The fallen spy designates an heir.

1428
01:10:20,414 --> 01:10:21,882
Through a final transfer,

1429
01:10:21,907 --> 01:10:24,777
every shred of intel
they've accumulated...

1430
01:10:24,801 --> 01:10:26,986
gets passed directly to the successor.

1431
01:10:28,121 --> 01:10:30,089
What kind of angle is this?

1432
01:10:30,223 --> 01:10:31,658
The guy is brilliant.

1433
01:10:32,125 --> 01:10:35,227
Here is the premise the spy operates on.

1434
01:10:35,462 --> 01:10:37,264
"I am not here to hunt people down."

1435
01:10:37,464 --> 01:10:39,126
"I have no desire for mass eliminations."

1436
01:10:39,527 --> 01:10:43,836
"That isn't how the game is played
in the sphere I operate in."

1437
01:10:44,905 --> 01:10:47,006
If you genuinely believe I'm the spy,

1438
01:10:47,007 --> 01:10:49,609
look back at everything
I've actually done so far.

1439
01:10:49,910 --> 01:10:51,545
Have people been dropping left and right?

1440
01:10:52,446 --> 01:10:54,014
He actually managed
to minimize casualties.

1441
01:10:54,381 --> 01:10:56,315
That's a solid defense.

1442
01:10:56,616 --> 01:10:57,616
Nice knowing you, Benjamin.

1443
01:10:57,617 --> 01:10:59,519
He's remarkable. He came totally prepared.

1444
01:11:00,854 --> 01:11:03,088
A spy operates by principles.

1445
01:11:03,089 --> 01:11:04,531
There was fierce pushback...

1446
01:11:04,555 --> 01:11:06,359
against arbitrarily targeting
innocent residents.

1447
01:11:06,360 --> 01:11:08,294
The guiding rule was simply...

1448
01:11:08,728 --> 01:11:10,936
that whoever stirred up toxic discord...

1449
01:11:10,961 --> 01:11:13,532
had to be prioritized for elimination.

1450
01:11:13,933 --> 01:11:15,902
And that's exactly what was carried out.

1451
01:11:19,573 --> 01:11:21,975
Being a spy is merely
a designated assignment.

1452
01:11:22,175 --> 01:11:24,711
The mission parameters might differ,

1453
01:11:25,044 --> 01:11:28,180
but that doesn't mean a spy lacks
integrity or breaks their word.

1454
01:11:28,348 --> 01:11:29,850
I can stand by that statement.

1455
01:11:31,051 --> 01:11:33,386
What was the guarantee given to Nangja?

1456
01:11:34,087 --> 01:11:35,555
"When we reach the final stage,"

1457
01:11:35,789 --> 01:11:36,756
"I will guarantee..."

1458
01:11:37,224 --> 01:11:38,691
"your path to victory."

1459
01:11:39,056 --> 01:11:40,559
Did he have full clearance
on the upcoming rules?

1460
01:11:40,560 --> 01:11:42,361
He might have received
the brief in advance.

1461
01:11:42,362 --> 01:11:44,130
Regardless, we still have to take him out.

1462
01:11:44,364 --> 01:11:45,497
Incredible bluff.

1463
01:11:45,498 --> 01:11:46,766
The bottom line is this.

1464
01:11:46,843 --> 01:11:49,645
Even if Nangja had survived
and taken the win,

1465
01:11:49,670 --> 01:11:52,172
this was never designed as a zero-sum game
where everyone else has to lose.

1466
01:11:52,706 --> 01:11:54,506
Don't kid yourselves into thinking...

1467
01:11:54,908 --> 01:11:57,910
you have full control over the board.

1468
01:11:59,780 --> 01:12:03,683
I urge you to make the rational choice
as a collective community.

1469
01:12:03,684 --> 01:12:06,085
- That is persuasive.
- I told you he's smart.

1470
01:12:06,419 --> 01:12:08,887
If you're dead set on branding me the spy,

1471
01:12:08,888 --> 01:12:11,823
you can freeze me out with zero votes
and send me packing.

1472
01:12:12,259 --> 01:12:15,061
But an unseen successor will
immediately take my place.

1473
01:12:15,062 --> 01:12:17,464
At least the next one
might not be as sharp as you.

1474
01:12:18,665 --> 01:12:20,165
That's true.

1475
01:12:20,333 --> 01:12:21,333
Furthermore,

1476
01:12:21,902 --> 01:12:24,436
you will never have another spy
willing to sit down...

1477
01:12:24,437 --> 01:12:25,972
and negotiate terms at the table.

1478
01:12:26,706 --> 01:12:27,908
He's openly threatening us.

1479
01:12:28,042 --> 01:12:29,508
Is he trying to intimidate the room?

1480
01:12:29,509 --> 01:12:31,076
That was a blatant threat.

1481
01:12:31,077 --> 01:12:32,646
He really knows
how to get under your skin.

1482
01:12:32,680 --> 01:12:34,213
What a complete waste of tears.

1483
01:12:34,948 --> 01:12:36,883
So Nangja was caught in a shakedown.

1484
01:12:37,017 --> 01:12:39,218
- Glad we voted her out then!
- This makes my blood boil.

1485
01:12:39,452 --> 01:12:40,252
I'm getting literal goosebumps.

1486
01:12:40,253 --> 01:12:41,869
What a disaster
if we'd actually spared her.

1487
01:12:42,356 --> 01:12:43,589
So now,

1488
01:12:43,590 --> 01:12:46,092
as a whole community,
we need to think this through.

1489
01:12:46,426 --> 01:12:50,196
Those communal values
you keep talking about.

1490
01:12:50,530 --> 01:12:52,231
What does he know about community values?

1491
01:12:52,232 --> 01:12:53,999
He is the one trying to tear
the community apart.

1492
01:12:54,000 --> 01:12:56,268
Think about what kind of spy...

1493
01:12:56,802 --> 01:12:58,805
you want to leave behind for this house.

1494
01:12:59,105 --> 01:13:00,005
Thank you.

1495
01:13:03,410 --> 01:13:05,045
I cannot believe...

1496
01:13:05,378 --> 01:13:08,780
he actually went there
and pulled that off.

1497
01:13:09,049 --> 01:13:10,549
My jaw literally dropped.

1498
01:13:10,884 --> 01:13:12,952
The guy is brilliant.

1499
01:13:12,953 --> 01:13:14,787
And infuriating.

1500
01:13:16,122 --> 01:13:17,991
He is completely insufferable.

1501
01:13:18,525 --> 01:13:21,026
If an anti-fan is still a fan, sign me up.

1502
01:13:21,728 --> 01:13:24,530
The fact that the role gets passed down
after elimination...

1503
01:13:24,764 --> 01:13:27,133
means our objective...

1504
01:13:27,134 --> 01:13:30,035
is not just to hunt down
and take out the spy.

1505
01:13:30,036 --> 01:13:32,905
It also means the spy's goal
is not merely...

1506
01:13:32,906 --> 01:13:34,506
to survive to the very end.

1507
01:13:34,507 --> 01:13:36,742
The role survives
regardless of who holds it.

1508
01:13:36,743 --> 01:13:39,478
Then what is the spy's actual purpose
in this game?

1509
01:13:39,479 --> 01:13:41,147
That is what I really want to know.

1510
01:13:42,249 --> 01:13:43,216
I genuinely...

1511
01:13:43,617 --> 01:13:45,451
wanted to strangle him.

1512
01:13:45,986 --> 01:13:49,488
Not literally, of course,

1513
01:13:49,956 --> 01:13:53,026
but him going,
"The character Ben is dead."

1514
01:13:53,093 --> 01:13:54,894
It was absolute nonsense.

1515
01:13:55,896 --> 01:13:57,596
He was openly mocking us.

1516
01:13:57,931 --> 01:14:00,499
Coming at us with a deal like this...

1517
01:14:00,967 --> 01:14:02,467
is a joke in itself.

1518
01:14:02,769 --> 01:14:05,838
There is no way in hell I am negotiating.

1519
01:14:06,706 --> 01:14:07,983
I thought he had lost his mind.

1520
01:14:08,008 --> 01:14:10,276
I was completely caught off guard.

1521
01:14:10,844 --> 01:14:11,945
He is a tactical genius.

1522
01:14:13,079 --> 01:14:14,813
"The Ben show is officially over."

1523
01:14:14,814 --> 01:14:16,581
That was his grand finale.

1524
01:14:16,582 --> 01:14:18,583
That was your final card?

1525
01:14:18,652 --> 01:14:20,686
Pulling that move was basically
game suicide,

1526
01:14:20,687 --> 01:14:23,555
yet it felt like he had planned out
every single beat.

1527
01:14:23,556 --> 01:14:27,359
I actually had to respect his commitment
to maintaining his dignity...

1528
01:14:27,360 --> 01:14:28,995
as a true villain right to the end.

1529
01:14:29,953 --> 01:14:30,963
You know the saying,

1530
01:14:30,964 --> 01:14:31,969
"If your time has come,"

1531
01:14:31,993 --> 01:14:34,876
"you might as well enjoy
one last cigarette."

1532
01:14:34,901 --> 01:14:35,701
It had that energy.

1533
01:14:35,702 --> 01:14:37,136
Even if you are going down,

1534
01:14:37,137 --> 01:14:38,771
you still take your final shot.

1535
01:14:38,772 --> 01:14:40,339
He is bargaining for his life...

1536
01:14:40,340 --> 01:14:42,741
using his last available card.

1537
01:14:42,742 --> 01:14:44,777
If the final game...

1538
01:14:44,778 --> 01:14:47,046
comes down to a prisoner's dilemma,

1539
01:14:47,247 --> 01:14:49,448
where two people choose
whether to share, steal everything,

1540
01:14:49,449 --> 01:14:51,016
or walk away with nothing,

1541
01:14:51,237 --> 01:14:54,540
then you could deliberately
pair up two reliable allies...

1542
01:14:54,565 --> 01:14:56,222
and guarantee they walk away
with the cash.

1543
01:14:56,223 --> 01:14:59,225
I originally planned to pair
Nangja with Dark Knight.

1544
01:14:59,226 --> 01:15:00,826
He would have trusted her
without hesitation.

1545
01:15:00,927 --> 01:15:02,394
If I pair someone up with him,

1546
01:15:02,395 --> 01:15:04,029
they essentially get a free ride
to the prize.

1547
01:15:04,030 --> 01:15:05,874
Even though she ended
up getting evicted anyway.

1548
01:15:05,899 --> 01:15:07,732
I always keep my promises.

1549
01:15:08,168 --> 01:15:10,569
That is the standard I operate by.

1550
01:15:11,338 --> 01:15:14,207
If you choose not to trust me, so be it.

1551
01:15:14,874 --> 01:15:17,109
(Life Speeches complete.)

1552
01:15:21,448 --> 01:15:23,416
- That was unbelievable.
- Amazing, Benjamin!

1553
01:15:23,417 --> 01:15:25,051
Since when has your Korean been so fluent?

1554
01:15:25,285 --> 01:15:26,352
Please keep me in!

1555
01:15:27,253 --> 01:15:28,386
- Hey.
- Please save me!

1556
01:15:28,387 --> 01:15:30,290
You are the one who took me out!

1557
01:15:30,323 --> 01:15:32,057
You are the one who eliminated me!

1558
01:15:32,058 --> 01:15:33,525
- Get back over there.
- Please save me!

1559
01:15:33,526 --> 01:15:34,626
The man is a genius.

1560
01:15:35,428 --> 01:15:36,296
An absolute genius.

1561
01:15:36,430 --> 01:15:37,964
Think about it logically.

1562
01:15:38,031 --> 01:15:39,599
Trust me on this.

1563
01:15:41,401 --> 01:15:43,535
(Life Speech voting begins.)

1564
01:15:43,536 --> 01:15:45,572
I guess nobody here actually
gets what I am doing.

1565
01:15:47,040 --> 01:15:49,308
You don't seem particularly
invested either.

1566
01:15:49,309 --> 01:15:51,177
You seem completely checked out.

1567
01:15:54,247 --> 01:15:56,248
This is a critical decision.

1568
01:15:56,616 --> 01:16:00,319
- Since money was what everyone...
- Don't fall for his pitch.

1569
01:16:00,320 --> 01:16:01,621
- Let's head over.
- I'm good.

1570
01:16:01,622 --> 01:16:03,689
- We should watch out for Genie.
- Don't listen, go!

1571
01:16:03,790 --> 01:16:06,092
Genie has been wavering since yesterday.

1572
01:16:08,628 --> 01:16:09,729
I thought she might pull a move,

1573
01:16:09,730 --> 01:16:10,696
but maybe not.

1574
01:16:11,364 --> 01:16:13,465
The tide has turned.

1575
01:16:16,102 --> 01:16:17,803
I'll be right back.

1576
01:16:21,074 --> 01:16:22,508
(Vote for the most moving speech.)

1577
01:16:22,509 --> 01:16:23,943
Time to cast votes.

1578
01:16:24,878 --> 01:16:27,112
Seriously, just vote to save me.

1579
01:16:27,447 --> 01:16:28,648
Why can't anyone think rationally?

1580
01:16:28,649 --> 01:16:30,383
You are letting emotions
cloud your judgment.

1581
01:16:30,583 --> 01:16:33,319
Did they pack this place exclusively
with humanities majors?

1582
01:16:36,456 --> 01:16:37,956
Let's cast our ballots already!

1583
01:16:38,591 --> 01:16:39,893
Let's go!

1584
01:16:41,561 --> 01:16:42,661
Did you submit your vote?

1585
01:16:42,662 --> 01:16:45,297
About to. For round two,

1586
01:16:46,032 --> 01:16:47,500
back me up on this.

1587
01:16:47,733 --> 01:16:49,301
- Round two?
- Why?

1588
01:16:49,302 --> 01:16:50,969
- It's Genie.
- Did something happen?

1589
01:16:50,970 --> 01:16:52,281
She asked me to back her.

1590
01:16:52,306 --> 01:16:54,406
- Don't vote for her.
- She promised to back me in return.

1591
01:16:54,407 --> 01:16:56,141
- Don't do it.
- What about you?

1592
01:16:56,142 --> 01:16:58,110
I'll vote for you, of course.

1593
01:16:58,111 --> 01:16:59,579
- Do you swear you'll vote for me?
- You have my word.

1594
01:16:59,580 --> 01:17:00,879
- Then you have mine.
- Deal.

1595
01:17:00,880 --> 01:17:01,981
I will let Genie know.

1596
01:17:01,982 --> 01:17:03,483
- Sounds good.
- Okay.

1597
01:17:03,484 --> 01:17:05,684
Genie already has immunity anyway.

1598
01:17:05,685 --> 01:17:06,852
Exactly my point.

1599
01:17:09,189 --> 01:17:10,456
Where is your vote going in round two?

1600
01:17:10,457 --> 01:17:11,837
We already agreed on Genie yesterday...

1601
01:17:11,862 --> 01:17:13,526
and she is likely ending up
in last place anyway, so...

1602
01:17:13,527 --> 01:17:15,194
Plus, she holds immunity,

1603
01:17:15,195 --> 01:17:17,029
so she will not be picking up many votes.
I just put her down,

1604
01:17:17,030 --> 01:17:19,131
and the rest of the ranks should
sort themselves out.

1605
01:17:19,132 --> 01:17:21,600
She holds immunity.

1606
01:17:21,601 --> 01:17:22,735
Right.

1607
01:17:22,736 --> 01:17:24,504
- Honestly speaking,
- She has immunity, so...

1608
01:17:24,505 --> 01:17:27,009
To be fair, I took a direct hit...

1609
01:17:27,010 --> 01:17:28,474
and got wiped out financially.

1610
01:17:28,776 --> 01:17:29,842
I have a solid case.

1611
01:17:29,843 --> 01:17:31,677
I think you take first place.

1612
01:17:31,678 --> 01:17:33,179
I am a man of integrity!

1613
01:17:33,580 --> 01:17:34,747
Pure integrity!

1614
01:17:35,348 --> 01:17:37,183
Nothing but cold, hard facts.

1615
01:17:37,884 --> 01:17:39,752
What perk did the journalist
end up getting?

1616
01:17:39,953 --> 01:17:41,720
I wanted to carry the journalist
all the way to the end...

1617
01:17:41,821 --> 01:17:43,755
and set things up...

1618
01:17:43,756 --> 01:17:46,426
so she would be positioned to win.

1619
01:17:47,060 --> 01:17:48,528
His arrogance is off the charts.

1620
01:17:48,761 --> 01:17:50,528
It's infuriating
how he looks down on everyone.

1621
01:17:50,529 --> 01:17:51,496
So obnoxious.

1622
01:17:51,764 --> 01:17:52,731
Unbelievable.

1623
01:17:53,500 --> 01:17:54,433
He really acts like...

1624
01:17:54,534 --> 01:17:55,834
a total karen3000.

1625
01:17:56,136 --> 01:17:57,670
- Exactly.
- I hate him.

1626
01:17:57,937 --> 01:17:58,970
Totally.

1627
01:18:00,006 --> 01:18:01,474
Why does he look so smug?

1628
01:18:02,175 --> 01:18:04,510
So am I hearing this right?

1629
01:18:04,844 --> 01:18:06,311
In theory,

1630
01:18:07,012 --> 01:18:09,081
if the spy gets eliminated,
a replacement steps in?

1631
01:18:09,082 --> 01:18:11,750
- Don't engage.
- Don't buy into it.

1632
01:18:12,585 --> 01:18:14,920
Without question.

1633
01:18:16,256 --> 01:18:17,289
That's precisely how it works.

1634
01:18:17,290 --> 01:18:19,858
"You fools, I see the whole board."

1635
01:18:19,859 --> 01:18:21,094
Why does any of that matter?

1636
01:18:21,494 --> 01:18:23,062
Our only job is
to secure the cash and get out.

1637
01:18:23,130 --> 01:18:24,497
That's what I have been saying all along.

1638
01:18:24,498 --> 01:18:27,134
Some people are still in denial,

1639
01:18:27,135 --> 01:18:28,401
but the reality will hit soon enough.

1640
01:18:30,937 --> 01:18:31,770
Still,

1641
01:18:32,705 --> 01:18:35,240
if the role truly transfers
upon elimination,

1642
01:18:35,241 --> 01:18:36,541
my money is on Genie taking the baton.

1643
01:18:36,542 --> 01:18:38,243
Tomorrow is the finale anyway.

1644
01:18:38,244 --> 01:18:40,280
Don't lose sleep
over who inherits the role.

1645
01:18:40,613 --> 01:18:42,181
Are you getting swayed by him again?

1646
01:18:42,482 --> 01:18:44,750
I bought into it for a second.

1647
01:18:44,751 --> 01:18:47,386
Could someone really inherit the spy role
with only a day left?

1648
01:18:47,387 --> 01:18:48,754
I could never pull that off.

1649
01:18:48,755 --> 01:18:49,889
This game...

1650
01:18:49,914 --> 01:18:52,091
just keeps throwing curveballs
right to the wire.

1651
01:19:05,205 --> 01:19:07,473
I just want to recover some of my losses.

1652
01:19:07,774 --> 01:19:11,710
The results of the Life Speeches
will be announced momentarily.

1653
01:19:12,011 --> 01:19:14,113
Let's stick to Korean.
English is too exhausting.

1654
01:19:16,115 --> 01:19:17,984
I hope every single one of you
falls flat tomorrow.

1655
01:19:18,151 --> 01:19:19,752
First, under the keyword "Hypocrite,"

1656
01:19:19,786 --> 01:19:23,455
the resident
with the highest vote count is...

1657
01:19:24,191 --> 01:19:27,359
Go Ae Shin.

1658
01:19:27,528 --> 01:19:29,595
(5,000 dollar prize)

1659
01:19:29,729 --> 01:19:30,796
Well deserved.

1660
01:19:30,797 --> 01:19:31,764
I'm so jealous.

1661
01:19:31,765 --> 01:19:35,100
The resident with the lowest
vote count, placing last, is...

1662
01:19:35,808 --> 01:19:38,009
Polar Bear.

1663
01:19:39,439 --> 01:19:41,307
- Good job.
- Only the normal ones are here.

1664
01:19:41,711 --> 01:19:43,842
He just can't stop babbling now.

1665
01:19:43,843 --> 01:19:47,379
For "Money", the resident
with the highest vote count is...

1666
01:19:47,380 --> 01:19:48,781
- Michael?
- Definitely Michael.

1667
01:19:48,782 --> 01:19:50,116
- Has to be Michael, right?
- Michael.

1668
01:19:50,416 --> 01:19:52,550
Banu.

1669
01:19:54,187 --> 01:19:55,454
You've won some hearts.

1670
01:19:55,455 --> 01:19:57,089
(5,000 dollar prize)

1671
01:19:57,090 --> 01:20:00,359
The resident
with the lowest vote count is...

1672
01:20:00,627 --> 01:20:03,062
Genie.

1673
01:20:03,751 --> 01:20:04,329
(Holds immunity)

1674
01:20:04,330 --> 01:20:06,331
Pretty much what we anticipated.

1675
01:20:07,533 --> 01:20:09,102
For the final keyword,

1676
01:20:09,535 --> 01:20:13,404
"Inferiority", the resident
with the highest vote count is...

1677
01:20:13,740 --> 01:20:16,908
Superman.

1678
01:20:16,909 --> 01:20:18,510
(5,000 dollar prize)

1679
01:20:19,979 --> 01:20:23,282
The resident
with the lowest vote count is...

1680
01:20:26,986 --> 01:20:30,289
Benjamin.

1681
01:20:32,959 --> 01:20:34,026
Take me out, then.

1682
01:20:34,193 --> 01:20:34,992
They choose three people.

1683
01:20:34,993 --> 01:20:36,561
One receives salvation,
the leader is safe,

1684
01:20:36,562 --> 01:20:37,862
and one gets the boot.

1685
01:20:38,031 --> 01:20:40,370
(The team leader is exempt
from elimination.)

1686
01:20:40,901 --> 01:20:42,868
So we don't need to cast a vote here.

1687
01:20:42,869 --> 01:20:44,003
That means...

1688
01:20:44,004 --> 01:20:45,971
(They placed Polar Bear last
for the "Hypocrite" speeches.)

1689
01:20:45,972 --> 01:20:48,140
(They placed Benjamin last
for the "Inferiority" speeches.)

1690
01:20:48,504 --> 01:20:50,275
(The leader saves the person
who placed last in "Money.")

1691
01:20:50,276 --> 01:20:52,044
You can still grant salvation.

1692
01:20:52,045 --> 01:20:53,112
Just pick one to save.

1693
01:20:53,313 --> 01:20:55,148
So this is the setup to take out Benjamin?

1694
01:20:55,149 --> 01:20:56,582
It's never just a speech contest...

1695
01:20:56,583 --> 01:20:57,783
(A strategy to eliminate Benjamin)

1696
01:20:57,784 --> 01:20:59,518
but an orchestrated strike against him.

1697
01:21:00,354 --> 01:21:04,890
Genie and Benjamin...

1698
01:21:05,358 --> 01:21:07,092
are officially up for eviction.

1699
01:21:07,296 --> 01:21:09,662
Seeing that graphic pop up is brutal.

1700
01:21:10,497 --> 01:21:14,600
Team leader Polar Bear is exempt
from elimination.

1701
01:21:15,501 --> 01:21:19,104
The leader will now choose
one resident to save.

1702
01:21:20,406 --> 01:21:22,041
Use that authority to save me, please.

1703
01:21:22,408 --> 01:21:25,977
You caught a lucky break.

1704
01:21:28,882 --> 01:21:30,582
This is so frustrating.

1705
01:21:30,607 --> 01:21:32,485
I can't get through to a bunch
of humanities majors...

1706
01:21:33,553 --> 01:21:35,288
who cannot even run simple probability.

1707
01:21:37,523 --> 01:21:39,491
What a poetic final meltdown.

1708
01:21:42,829 --> 01:21:45,513
(Select the resident
you would like to save.)

1709
01:21:45,798 --> 01:21:48,833
(Genie)

1710
01:21:49,302 --> 01:21:50,869
I am saving Genie.

1711
01:21:50,870 --> 01:21:52,238
See you around, Benjamin.

1712
01:21:54,707 --> 01:21:57,210
Today's evictee has been determined.

1713
01:21:57,643 --> 01:22:00,878
Benjamin,

1714
01:22:01,347 --> 01:22:05,350
please exit the Community House
immediately.

1715
01:22:07,720 --> 01:22:09,154
Have fun out there, everyone.

1716
01:22:09,155 --> 01:22:10,423
Take care.

1717
01:22:13,626 --> 01:22:14,526
Catch you on the outside.

1718
01:22:14,527 --> 01:22:15,928
Sorry about draining your prize pool.

1719
01:22:16,062 --> 01:22:17,864
Not like I can pack it
into my suitcase anyway.

1720
01:22:21,610 --> 01:22:23,436
Goodbye, everyone.
I hope you all crash and burn.

1721
01:22:28,941 --> 01:22:31,242
What on earth was that?

1722
01:22:33,045 --> 01:22:34,280
The guy is unreal.

1723
01:22:45,391 --> 01:22:47,159
Spending a week living as Benjamin...

1724
01:22:47,860 --> 01:22:50,261
showed me what I was capable of.

1725
01:22:50,763 --> 01:22:53,332
Once a cheater, always a cheater.

1726
01:22:54,033 --> 01:22:55,101
It was a blast.

1727
01:22:55,401 --> 01:22:56,201
It really was.

1728
01:22:56,969 --> 01:22:59,404
Where else could anyone
pull off something like that?

1729
01:22:59,672 --> 01:23:01,039
Under normal circumstances,

1730
01:23:01,651 --> 01:23:03,185
nobody pretends to be an expat like that.

1731
01:23:03,210 --> 01:23:05,644
Other roles wouldn't have been
half as entertaining.

1732
01:23:05,978 --> 01:23:08,680
I have zero interest
in being just another lawyer...

1733
01:23:08,714 --> 01:23:10,081
squabbling with politicians.

1734
01:23:10,883 --> 01:23:12,918
What resonated with me
was the community itself.

1735
01:23:13,353 --> 01:23:15,987
Seeing people show genuine care
for one another...

1736
01:23:16,122 --> 01:23:17,689
and recognizing that mutual trust
lifts everyone up.

1737
01:23:17,790 --> 01:23:19,791
Unfortunately, reality
doesn't run on those rules.

1738
01:23:19,992 --> 01:23:21,960
That is politics for you.

1739
01:23:22,228 --> 01:23:23,161
That's the mission.

1740
01:23:24,230 --> 01:23:27,332
You can't run a country
with only people like this.

1741
01:23:27,609 --> 01:23:28,976
From a larger population,

1742
01:23:29,001 --> 01:23:32,136
we deliberately isolated a small group.

1743
01:23:32,371 --> 01:23:34,906
There aren't really people
you can call spies in society.

1744
01:23:34,907 --> 01:23:36,074
Everyone just lives together.

1745
01:23:36,275 --> 01:23:37,742
Even people who've committed crimes.

1746
01:23:37,743 --> 01:23:41,479
Our system isn't designed
to label someone...

1747
01:23:42,247 --> 01:23:44,382
as impure and exile them from society.

1748
01:23:44,383 --> 01:23:45,984
That's not its purpose.

1749
01:23:45,985 --> 01:23:47,118
At least, that's how I see it.

1750
01:23:47,119 --> 01:23:49,220
Emotionally, I completely
understand the reaction.

1751
01:23:49,356 --> 01:23:52,260
But branding someone as tainted
and saying, "Just turn a blind eye,"

1752
01:23:52,261 --> 01:23:54,493
doesn't actually solve the problem.

1753
01:23:55,093 --> 01:23:57,362
Yet here in this community,

1754
01:23:57,363 --> 01:23:59,165
the spy didn't actually do anything.

1755
01:23:59,432 --> 01:24:00,600
It was merely their existence.

1756
01:24:00,699 --> 01:24:03,301
Just planting the thought of a spy
in everyone's minds...

1757
01:24:03,302 --> 01:24:06,304
turned them against each other
and bred conflict.

1758
01:24:06,405 --> 01:24:08,973
Even when you try to help
right up to the end,

1759
01:24:08,974 --> 01:24:10,918
people reject it
because of their prejudice.

1760
01:24:10,943 --> 01:24:13,811
They'd rather label someone as tainted
and shut them out entirely.

1761
01:24:13,812 --> 01:24:15,490
That's something we really
need to reflect on.

1762
01:24:15,515 --> 01:24:17,449
I always wanted to help you guys.

1763
01:24:17,450 --> 01:24:20,118
I wanted you all to succeed in the end.

1764
01:24:20,320 --> 01:24:21,920
I hope you all make a lot of money.

1765
01:24:22,021 --> 01:24:25,523
Thank you for leaving Benjamin alone.

1766
01:24:25,918 --> 01:24:27,593
The introverted Benjamin...

1767
01:24:28,294 --> 01:24:29,729
can return to my imagination...

1768
01:24:29,829 --> 01:24:31,530
and never come out again.

1769
01:24:32,598 --> 01:24:33,531
Thank you.

1770
01:24:35,835 --> 01:24:37,637
- Should we step outside for some air?
- Yeah?

1771
01:24:38,210 --> 01:24:40,311
I'm suffocating in here.

1772
01:24:42,508 --> 01:24:44,573
He really pissed me off at the end there.

1773
01:24:44,598 --> 01:24:46,945
He was totally mocking the game.

1774
01:24:47,480 --> 01:24:49,915
- Right to the finish.
- Why is he like that?

1775
01:24:50,449 --> 01:24:52,917
He probably hasn't run
into many people sharper than him.

1776
01:24:53,853 --> 01:24:58,089
Still, being smart doesn't give you
a pass to be arrogant.

1777
01:24:58,524 --> 01:25:01,617
Whining that he lost
to humanities majors...

1778
01:25:01,642 --> 01:25:04,163
who can't even do basic math is absurd.

1779
01:25:05,064 --> 01:25:06,240
He actually had the nerve to say,

1780
01:25:06,265 --> 01:25:08,166
"Catch you on the outside,"
as he walked out.

1781
01:25:08,367 --> 01:25:11,203
We shouldn't have let on
how badly we wanted him gone.

1782
01:25:13,973 --> 01:25:18,310
He was spouting absolute nonsense
about Guam and the tourism scene.

1783
01:25:19,812 --> 01:25:21,646
Didn't he literally sketch out
a whole map of the island?

1784
01:25:21,647 --> 01:25:22,447
Yeah.

1785
01:25:22,448 --> 01:25:23,749
"If Guam sits here,"

1786
01:25:23,849 --> 01:25:25,583
"then right down this way..."

1787
01:25:25,851 --> 01:25:26,917
The whole thing was made up.

1788
01:25:26,918 --> 01:25:28,954
He did all that just to win our trust.

1789
01:25:29,088 --> 01:25:30,088
Ridiculous.

1790
01:25:31,557 --> 01:25:32,792
Absolutely.

1791
01:25:33,059 --> 01:25:34,960
- How old is he anyway?
- Born in '91.

1792
01:25:36,963 --> 01:25:39,198
I can't help but swear.

1793
01:25:39,565 --> 01:25:41,367
We're 11 years apart.

1794
01:25:42,635 --> 01:25:44,804
I hope I never cross paths with him
in the real world.

1795
01:25:57,950 --> 01:25:58,816
What?

1796
01:26:03,508 --> 01:26:05,876
(Gray, you've been designated
as the new spy.)

1797
01:26:10,022 --> 01:26:11,130
Just as he said.

1798
01:26:12,732 --> 01:26:13,932
That bastard.

1799
01:26:15,468 --> 01:26:17,536
He hands me the spy role,

1800
01:26:17,537 --> 01:26:19,704
then has the audacity to ask me
to buy him drinks...

1801
01:26:19,705 --> 01:26:21,306
if I walk away with a huge payout.

1802
01:26:21,874 --> 01:26:23,041
Make a ton of money.

1803
01:26:23,509 --> 01:26:26,778
My shot at thirteen grand
vanished in a single second.

1804
01:26:26,779 --> 01:26:29,148
I hope he walks away
with far more than what he lost.

1805
01:26:29,214 --> 01:26:31,283
Not out of any personal affection for him,

1806
01:26:31,450 --> 01:26:33,551
it's just that this outcome would deal...

1807
01:26:33,552 --> 01:26:35,186
the heaviest blow to The Community.

1808
01:26:35,187 --> 01:26:37,388
Since he already pulled
off one rogue play,

1809
01:26:37,389 --> 01:26:38,923
I want to see him spark total chaos...

1810
01:26:38,924 --> 01:26:40,793
with an even more outrageous move.

1811
01:26:40,960 --> 01:26:43,662
Why can't people just trust each other?

1812
01:26:44,396 --> 01:26:45,330
The Community...

1813
01:26:45,331 --> 01:26:47,766
gets the exact spy it deserves.

1814
01:26:48,734 --> 01:26:51,402
(Make a ton of money, Benjamin.)

1815
01:26:56,208 --> 01:27:00,611
(Gray, the second spy)

1816
01:27:01,714 --> 01:27:05,017
He handed me this poisoned chalice
on his way out the door.

1817
01:27:05,485 --> 01:27:07,285
It felt like he was saying,
"Here, let's see you try."

1818
01:27:07,286 --> 01:27:08,729
Tossing this grenade
at my feet as he left.

1819
01:27:08,754 --> 01:27:11,023
I guess the sincerity I showed him...

1820
01:27:11,024 --> 01:27:12,924
ended up paying off in a twisted way.

1821
01:27:12,925 --> 01:27:14,760
Since we're heading
into the final stretch,

1822
01:27:14,761 --> 01:27:17,829
everyone will probably take
off the gloves and fight dirty.

1823
01:27:18,057 --> 01:27:20,132
From what I've gathered,

1824
01:27:20,157 --> 01:27:25,380
I get to determine tomorrow's
1-on-1 mission matchup.

1825
01:27:25,405 --> 01:27:27,572
If things play out the way I expect,

1826
01:27:27,840 --> 01:27:30,931
I think I have a solid shot
at over 50 percent of the money.

1827
01:27:30,955 --> 01:27:32,610
Maybe 75 percent.

1828
01:27:33,112 --> 01:27:34,446
It's my time to shine.

1829
01:27:35,781 --> 01:27:37,482
- I'm furious.
- Same here.

1830
01:27:38,017 --> 01:27:39,217
I was...

1831
01:27:39,218 --> 01:27:41,953
unjustly taken out,

1832
01:27:43,489 --> 01:27:45,157
yet nobody seemed to give a damn.

1833
01:27:45,718 --> 01:27:46,618
It's...

1834
01:27:46,792 --> 01:27:48,593
They said the second round
would be an open vote.

1835
01:27:48,594 --> 01:27:49,527
Right.

1836
01:27:49,930 --> 01:27:53,031
So I'm not feeling great
about this at all.

1837
01:27:54,066 --> 01:27:57,136
They keep preaching community spirit
while doing nothing to back it up.

1838
01:27:57,970 --> 01:27:59,771
Both of them.

1839
01:28:00,706 --> 01:28:02,106
Genie included.

1840
01:28:02,608 --> 01:28:05,576
All this time, everyone was grinding
to flush out the spy.

1841
01:28:05,577 --> 01:28:07,678
- That part is true.
- They put in all that work.

1842
01:28:08,741 --> 01:28:10,309
People even took a bullet for it.

1843
01:28:10,483 --> 01:28:12,184
Despite giving it their all,

1844
01:28:12,985 --> 01:28:15,154
mutual trust in this house
is now completely shattered.

1845
01:28:22,194 --> 01:28:23,996
What crushed me the most was...

1846
01:28:24,317 --> 01:28:25,931
I'm keeping my eyes locked
on the hypocrites...

1847
01:28:25,932 --> 01:28:28,166
who burst into tears
over missing out on Peking duck.

1848
01:28:29,969 --> 01:28:33,505
The final favorability vote
will begin shortly.

1849
01:28:33,506 --> 01:28:36,007
The bottom two will be up for eviction.

1850
01:28:36,008 --> 01:28:38,777
You're allowed to vote for yourself.

1851
01:28:38,778 --> 01:28:40,712
Each additional ballot costs...

1852
01:28:40,713 --> 01:28:43,448
one thousand dollars
out of your personal earnings.

1853
01:28:43,449 --> 01:28:46,051
If you want to stay alive,
you have to keep burning your own cash.

1854
01:28:46,052 --> 01:28:48,320
Dropping ten extra votes
runs you ten grand.

1855
01:28:48,321 --> 01:28:49,888
It turns into a total arms race.

1856
01:28:49,889 --> 01:28:51,856
If we walk away empty-handed,
it's all pointless.

1857
01:28:51,857 --> 01:28:53,592
- So how much do you have left?
- Twenty-four.

1858
01:28:53,593 --> 01:28:55,460
- I'm sitting on 16.
- I have 21.

1859
01:28:55,461 --> 01:28:57,862
Every single one of them is lying.
It's so exhausting.

1860
01:28:58,064 --> 01:28:59,565
Today's evictee is...

1861
01:29:02,167 --> 01:29:03,501
"The Dilemma Game."

1862
01:29:03,969 --> 01:29:05,571
"Two residents are paired up,"

1863
01:29:05,596 --> 01:29:09,074
"and they must choose whether to split
the total prize pot down the middle..."

1864
01:29:09,099 --> 01:29:11,342
"or try to steal the entire bag
for themselves."

1865
01:29:11,677 --> 01:29:13,645
Exactly as I suspected.
